WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.833
(music)

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.070
- This is definitely the
sort of seat of thanks

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.238
and so I want to echo
some of the thank-yous

00:00:13.238 --> 00:00:18.030
that were made earlier. To
the Lewis Walpole library

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.080
for being utterly
formative in my second book

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.470
and it's a kind of ongoing relationship

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:24.630
for which I'm very grateful.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:25.990
I especially want to thank Cindy

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.600
for asking me to direct
"The Mysterious Mother."

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.970
While I have a few 18th
century restoration comedies

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.610
under my belt, I had never
tried my hand at tragedy

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.960
and she both trusted me with it

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.040
and kind of talked me into it

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.880
and I'm so so glad you did.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.300
I also want to do one other thank you

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.960
for someone who has been
for me in this project

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.120
behind the scenes and a huge
help and that's Joe Roach.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.960
Joe challenged me about seven years ago

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.430
to do The Scholarship of Performance

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.250
and it was one of those suggestions

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.310
that was utterly transformative
for my professional life

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.470
and my intellectual life
and my students lives.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.520
So I just want to thank you for that Joe.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.280
It's definitely enhanced
my ongoing love affair

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.393
with the 18th century.

00:01:08.239 --> 00:01:11.040
All right well when we started rehearsals

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.593
for "The Mysterious Mother" lo
these many three days ago,

00:01:16.371 --> 00:01:17.530
the first thing I said to the cast

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.998
is "the only way out of
this play is over the top."

00:01:20.998 --> 00:01:25.998
We were faced with a less than 48-hour

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.970
start to finish process

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.660
and a play that is genuinely interesting

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.930
and so that over-the-top
has played itself out

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.100
in a few different ways.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.890
For those of you who
weren't in on the secret,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.620
the two evil priests were
played by an actual priest

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.163
and a Catholic theologian.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.360
We also went to some
pains mostly Cindy's pains

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.190
to try to reproduce the
Beauclerk illustrations

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.890
in our lavish costuming which included

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.450
a little gift from the universe

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.670
when we got Florian's costume back;

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.130
that beautiful purple costume.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.500
We were told it was insured for extra

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.544
and it's a very beautiful costume

00:02:05.544 --> 00:02:08.280
because it was worn by a famous person.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.820
When he took it off the
rack and looked in the tag,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.620
it said Mr. Lane.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.637
It was worn by Nathan
Lane sometime in the 70s.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.560
So everything here is over-the-top.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.010
Even the Nathan Lane
connection. And finally

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.510
it felt over the top or
puckish or downright heretical

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.030
to be projecting images
of this Gothic castle

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.620
on Louis Kahn's clean, modernist wall

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.530
and see it works so well.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.810
So the cast has already
been working on the musical.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.280
We rehearsed "Mama Mia"
last night after the show

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.950
and we have a social media campaign

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.557
it's #Who'syourdaddy.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.660
So this panel is about
"Staging 'The Mysterious Mother'"

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.570
and in the process that
we just went through

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:57.847
I think we experienced
some of the negotiations

00:02:57.847 --> 00:03:00.640
that earlier panelists
have already talked about.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.917
Our first negotiation
was sort of with the seen

00:03:02.917 --> 00:03:05.400
and the unseen in this play

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.720
and that's reflected in
the way we were negotiating

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.070
David's brilliant cutback
with the original text

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:16.380
and the things that we could
not play but tried to show you

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.653
or let you hear.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.360
We also were I think always
negotiating that line

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.090
between camp and tragedy
which turned out to be

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.860
a really productive
space and we found things

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.400
that we didn't expect to find.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.070
So we hope that that inquiry

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:34.150
will continue with these papers.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.897
So first we will hear from Marcie Frank.

00:03:36.897 --> 00:03:40.910
Marcie is a professor of
English at Concordia in Montreal

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.480
and she teaches mainly
18th century literature.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.240
She's finishing a book, "The Novel Stage

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.530
from the Comedy of Manners to Melodrama

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.700
which is about the ways
that theatrical conventions

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.380
shaped the novel between the
restoration and Jane Austen.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.120
Her most recent essay, on
contemporary American queer writer

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.800
Dennis Cooper, "Cooper's Queer Objects,"

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.590
--now I have to go read this now--

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.379
can be read in the
current issue of "Angelica."

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.980
(mumbles)

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.860
Alright that's my bid and the
title of her talk today is

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.450
"'Wilful Walpole: Performing Publication

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:18.700
in 'The Mysterious Mother'."

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.300
- Hi and thanks for coming
and thanks for having me

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.370
to Jonathan Kramnik and Jill Campbell

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:36.810
and I'm delighted to be here
to share my work with you

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:40.199
and I really enjoyed the staging

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:43.233
and we'll see what you think about this.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.980
The mystery of the Countess of Narbonne,

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.420
the mysterious mother
for whom Horace Walpole's

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.290
gothic tragedy is named,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.290
is established at the outset
of the play by elaborate,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:58.750
varied and even contradictory
discussion of her motivations

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.590
before she appears.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:03.330
The porter describes her virtuous piety

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.230
and the priests Martin and Benedict

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.490
discuss her proud secrecy.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.450
If her self-reliant despair
gives her some dignity

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.280
when she first comes on
stage at the end of Act one,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.840
Florian's reflections on her
treatment of her son Edmund

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:19.520
at the beginning of Act two,

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.030
complicate without rendering
comprehensible her character.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:26.000
He even depicts her mourning
the death of her husband

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.920
as hypocritical, "sable
chambers, the winking lamp,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.650
the pomp of midnight woe"
are but a specious theatre

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.380
on which th'inconstant mind with decency

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.873
forgets its inward tribute."

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.610
Language that resonates
with "The Castle of Otranto"

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:46.280
introduces his ironic question.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.270
"Who can doubt the love
which to a father's shade

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.140
devotes the son?"

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.300
The answer "The Mysterious Mother"

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.500
who has banished that son and determined

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.483
that he can only enjoy
his inheritance from afar.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.470
Holy shades of Hamlet Batman

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:06.380
for Florian, Edmund's Robin the male line

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.900
has been interrupted by
the excessive performance

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.783
a female grief set in a specious theater.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.450
The mystery of "The Mysterious
Mother" is sustained

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.260
until the play's end when the
countess reveals to Edmund

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.140
that his marriage to
Adeliza is incestuous

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.300
not only because she is his sister

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.350
but also because she is his daughter,

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.450
the offspring of a sexual encounter,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.770
he unbeknownst to himself had
enjoyed with his own mother

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.470
who disguised herself
as his beloved Beatrice

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:36.000
on the very night of her husband's death.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.360
The countess then kills herself.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:41.340
Though she initially inspires
pity and Martin and Benedict

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.640
who schemed to acquire her
property for the church,

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:47.290
invite skeptical distrust,
when her crime is revealed

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:50.180
in "A moment" as Walpole himself

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.537
described it in the PostScript.

00:06:52.537 --> 00:06:56.073
"A most odious moment
of depraved imagination"

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.860
the play is evacuated
of any internal ground

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.320
of moral judgment for the dark reversal

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.520
does not restore the
respectability of the church.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.410
Walpole knew that the double incest

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.640
would render the play unperformable

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.540
yet he nevertheless
adapted it to the scene.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.280
He exhibited a similar
ambivalence about its publication.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.170
Today I want to suggest
that the specious theater

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.690
of "The Mysterious Mother"

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.060
with its ambiguous relation to performance

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.270
and it's strange publication history

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:28.523
provides us with the template
for Walpole's other endeavors.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.670
Walpole recognized that the suppression

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:32.860
of the countess's secret

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:35.480
was essential to the
whole design of the play.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.680
As he observed in the PostScript
for the addition of 50

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.490
he initially printed at
Strawberry Hill in 1768

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.487
in order "To palliate the crime

00:07:44.487 --> 00:07:46.497
"and raise the character of the criminal,

00:07:46.497 --> 00:07:49.687
"the audience must be
prejudiced in her favor.

00:07:49.687 --> 00:07:52.097
"For this reason," he further explained,

00:07:52.097 --> 00:07:54.357
"I suppress the story till the last scene

00:07:54.357 --> 00:07:58.647
"and bestowed every ornament
of sense unbigoted piety,

00:07:58.647 --> 00:08:01.027
"an interesting contrition
on the character

00:08:01.027 --> 00:08:04.417
"that was at last to raise
Universal indignation."

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.210
Singling out some features of the play

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.237
for critical appreciation,

00:08:09.237 --> 00:08:12.527
"Terror and pity naturally
arise from the subject,

00:08:12.527 --> 00:08:15.007
"the moral is just and it conforms

00:08:15.007 --> 00:08:17.250
"to the three neoclassical unities,"

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.030
he nevertheless distinguished his desire

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:22.630
"of striking a little
out of the common road

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.860
to introduce some novelty on to our stage."

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.890
He risked the disgust he
knew the play would provoke

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.500
and though he apologized for it,

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.450
he also implied it was justified

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:34.600
for the introduction of the new character

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:36.530
of the Mysterious Mother.

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:39.750
He echoed these thoughts
in "Thoughts on Tragedy"

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.440
composed in 1775 but only published

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:45.770
in the posthumous complete works of 1798.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.730
The thoughts take the
form of three letters

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.600
to Robert Jephson who's
play "The Count of Narbonne"

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.600
was a successful adaptation
of "The Castle of Otranto"

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.900
that as its title suggests amalgamated

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.220
elements of "The Mysterious Mother."

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:02.760
It premiered in 1781 and was
performed 37 times up to 1798.

00:09:04.427 --> 00:09:06.027
"If there is any merit in my play,

00:09:06.027 --> 00:09:08.867
"I think it is in interrupting
the spectators fathoming

00:09:08.867 --> 00:09:12.067
"the whole story till the
last and in making every scene

00:09:12.067 --> 00:09:14.990
"tend to advance the catastrophe."

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.500
Exhibiting a self-consciousness

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.430
about his strengths and
weaknesses as a playwright,

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:23.050
Walpole remarked on his
attraction to the topic of incest

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.150
and passed on a story idea

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:29.420
for one of his favorite
subjects, Don Carlos.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.437
As Walpole explained to Jephson,

00:09:31.437 --> 00:09:34.237
"Why I gave up this fruitful canvas

00:09:34.237 --> 00:09:37.237
"was merely because the
passion is incestuous

00:09:37.237 --> 00:09:40.997
"as is most unfortunately
that of my Mysterious Mother

00:09:40.997 --> 00:09:44.017
"though at different points
in time and that of Carlos

00:09:44.017 --> 00:09:46.387
"a pardonable and not disgusting one,

00:09:46.387 --> 00:09:50.067
"I shall rejoice at having
left it if you will adopt it."

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:54.810
Suggesting that Jephson
adopt a story less

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.740
controversial than his own, Walpole here

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.720
encouraged his protégé by his own example.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.250
Evidently Walpole relished his
provocative effects on others

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:05.740
and here I'm going to recapitulate

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.640
some of the publication history

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.973
that we heard about
from Dale this morning.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.170
In the short preface he wrote
for the edition of the play,

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:17.499
he let Dodsley print in London in 1781

00:10:17.499 --> 00:10:22.499
which he claimed to be
publishing only to prevent piracy

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:26.287
Walpole again warded
off blame with apology.

00:10:26.287 --> 00:10:28.917
"He cannot be more blamed
than he blames himself

00:10:28.917 --> 00:10:31.577
"for having undertaken
so disagreeable a story

00:10:31.577 --> 00:10:33.347
"and for hazarding the publicity

00:10:33.347 --> 00:10:35.323
"by letting it go out of his hands"

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.590
Peter Sabor has observed
that Walpole's vexed attitude

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:42.500
toward publication is
taken to a compulsive level

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:44.030
with "The Mysterious Mother."

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.940
He desired to circulate in
print and to withhold himself

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:49.290
from such circulation by means of which

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.640
he could control critical response.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:56.520
The preface to Dodsley's edition
is dated April 29, 1781.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.050
Two letters of later
that year, one to Conway

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:03.340
dated May 28th and the other
two Mason dated July 3rd

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.470
indicate that though notice of publication

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:09.634
prevented the spurious
edition, it went ahead anyways.

00:11:09.634 --> 00:11:12.330
Walpole however retained the edition

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.570
and circulated copies to friends

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.700
as he had also done with the 1768 edition

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.223
printed at Strawberry Hill.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:23.250
Jephson's "The Count of Narbonne"
was printed by Thomas Cadell

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.300
right after its premiere
with the dedication

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:30.300
from Jephson to Walpole
dated November 17th, 1781.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.390
I'm here floating a speculation

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.570
that even though Walpole
didn't say so himself,

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.130
perhaps he sought to coordinate

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:39.580
the printing of his own tragedy

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.990
with the performance of Jephson's play.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.500
He was involved with Jephson's
production in other ways

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.003
including having loaned Alfonso's armor.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.930
Even though he didn't circulate
the publication of the play,

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:55.930
he may have wanted to have
some printed copies available

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.473
to restimulate interest in coordination

00:12:00.473 --> 00:12:03.860
with the production of Jephson's play.

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:05.530
Walpole was right to be concerned

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:08.080
about the reactions
his play would produce.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.040
Francis Burney recorded her disgust

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.160
upon the discovery of
the countess's secret

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.460
when she participated in a
closeted reading of the play.

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:18.847
She felt "A sort of indignant aversion

00:12:18.847 --> 00:12:20.767
"rise fast and warm in my mind

00:12:20.767 --> 00:12:25.007
"against the willful author
of a story so horrible.

00:12:25.007 --> 00:12:27.247
"All the entertainment
and pleasure I received

00:12:27.247 --> 00:12:30.910
"from Mr. Walpole seemed
extinguished by his lecture."

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:32.520
Burney's strong response

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.190
as her narrative of her
reading experience shows

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.670
derives from our initial
enjoyment of the play

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.260
and the interest it elicited

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:41.210
in the character of the countess.

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:44.500
Her indignant aversion bespeaks
a betrayal of expectations

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.107
which she directs against the

00:12:46.107 --> 00:12:49.450
"Willful author of a story so horrible."

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:52.210
Both Walpole's consistent
reflections on the disgust

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:55.190
the play must induce and Burney's response

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:59.100
indicate that the play's
streamline structure

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.010
was integral to its design.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.030
It's smooth acceleration
made its ultimate revelation

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.130
all the more offensive.

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.680
Though many critics understand

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.810
Walpole's native English
gothic to be opposed

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.540
to a neoclassicism
associated with the French,

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:16.800
Walpole who's francophilia is complex

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.190
but there demonstrates a
strategic neoclassicism

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:21.950
that serves to manage innovation

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.360
while maintaining audience appeal.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.810
In the PostScript he
minimized his investment

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.350
in critical approbation of the play,

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.920
for abiding by the rules
even though he himself

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.627
introduced those features for appraise.

00:13:34.627 --> 00:13:36.747
"If the critics be pleased
that this conformity

00:13:36.747 --> 00:13:40.037
"to their laws with this
conformity to their laws,

00:13:40.037 --> 00:13:43.207
"I shall be glad to
have their satisfaction

00:13:43.207 --> 00:13:47.167
"though for my own part I set
little value on such merit

00:13:47.167 --> 00:13:49.437
"which was accidental and at best mechanic

00:13:49.437 --> 00:13:52.617
"and of a subordinate kind
and were apt to produce

00:13:52.617 --> 00:13:54.610
"improbable situations that remove them."

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:57.480
So this sort of typical double gesture

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:02.480
that lets him praise himself
and then not take it away.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.460
An ideal venue for self-expression

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:08.130
and the manipulation of
response in many respects,

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.700
"The Mysterious Mother" occasioned

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.473
an emblematic performance
of Walpole's authorship.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.560
Theater pervaded Walpole's authorship

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:17.906
can be seen across his writing.

00:14:17.906 --> 00:14:19.590
The Castle of Otranto entered the world

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:22.140
as Walpole's production
under Shakespeare's mantle

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:24.440
and Marshall Brown has described it as

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:28.037
"a formal transmutation
of restoration tragedy."
303
00:14:28,037 --&gt; 00:14:32,860
Walpole's treatment of his 1773
comedy, Nature Will Prevail

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:34.500
mirrors that of his tragedy.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:37.720
He sent it to George
Colman at Covent Garden

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.010
who "Pressed to have it enlarged"

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:41.590
but then as the short notes

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:44.060
on the life of Horatio Walpole record,

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:46.377
he "Would not take the trouble to do so

00:14:46.377 --> 00:14:50.270
"for so slight and
extemporary a performance."

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:54.110
Earlier in his career and
Dale also talked about

00:14:55.488 --> 00:14:58.580
these forays into a
theatrical composition,

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:01.480
he had written "The Dear
Witches," a political pamphlet

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:05.210
in dramatic form that parodies
the witches scene in Macbeth.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.690
It was laid out with a
list of dramatis personae,

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:11.020
five scene divisions and
scene and stage directions

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.130
when it was published in the
Whig journal "Old England."

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:17.870
He also enjoyed his own
capacity to produce disgust

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:21.300
in tale four of the
Hieroglyphic Tales for example

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:25.160
in which the Archbishop of
Tuum drinks an aborted fetus

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:27.473
he had mistaken for a peach in brandy.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.910
Critics have long agreed
that Walpole's oeuvre

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:35.710
exhibits a remarkable consistency
across genres and media

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:38.630
including architecture,
gardening and collecting

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:41.250
which has led to psycho
biographical reflections

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:43.660
and disquisitions on taste.

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:46.670
Recently Sean Silver has
proposed that the design of

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:49.190
and writing about the
villa at Strawberry Hill

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:52.720
are emblematic of Walpole's
understanding of history.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.850
Proposing that the villa
served as a sort of

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:58.500
enlarged wunderkammer, Silver
turns into the experiences

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.770
Walpole sought to supply his visitors

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.820
though he doesn't bring
out the connections between

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:06.670
historiography as a grand
house tour and the theater.

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:08.920
Building on Silver's account, James Lilley

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:11.830
brilliantly grasps the
effective intensities

00:16:11.830 --> 00:16:14.910
and strenuous demands of
Walpole's double conception

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.740
of uniquity as collectible.

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:21.740
The singularity with no
intrinsic value that is common

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.520
and may even have been
produced more than once

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:26.740
as in an old Roman shoe whose worth

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:31.210
is independent of its former
wearer and the rare curiosity

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:32.940
of a monstrous birth.

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.400
Both operate on the margins of the market

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:38.620
remaining collectible only
to the discerning eye.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:41.000
The specious theater of
"The Mysterious Mother"

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.610
conforms to both aspects of uniquity.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:47.250
Depicting the suspension
of the patrilineal lines

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.210
so that the disgusting
generativity of the maternal

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:52.480
can be revealed and then expunged,

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:55.713
the play's subject matter
is monstrous birth.

00:16:55.713 --> 00:16:59.890
Unperformable and yet in
intermittent circulation,

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:02.330
the play's singularity is redoubled,

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:05.340
guaranteed by its appearances in print.

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:07.810
As "The Mysterious Mother"
brings out more clearly

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:11.900
than any other example, "Wilful
Walpole" enjoyed provoking

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:15.810
and amusing his coterie and
then sought to extend the circle

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:18.720
to strangers, provided that they prove

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.621
their eligibility for mentorship
by withstanding his tests,

00:17:22.621 --> 00:17:26.650
tests that included their
capacity to enjoy disgust

00:17:26.650 --> 00:17:29.180
and to admire triviality.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:31.430
(applause)

00:17:37.385 --> 00:17:39.960
- Thank you so much for that paper Marcie.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:43.030
Our second speaker will be Jean Marsden

00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:44.840
who's a professor of English

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:46.730
at the University of Connecticut.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.920
She's the author of
several books including

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.560
"The Reimagined Text,
Shakespeare Adaptation,

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:55.430
an 18th century literary
theory," "Fatal Desire,

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:58.590
Women's Sexuality in the English Stage"

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.750
and "Theaters of Feeling,
Affect and Performance

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:04.540
on the 18th Century Stage."

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:08.093
This last one forthcoming,
here be notified.

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:12.760
She is also the associate editor
of the works of Anne Finch

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:14.950
as well as numerous
articles on restoration

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:16.530
and 18th century theater.

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:20.810
Her title today is "Family
Dramas, 'The Mysterious Mother'

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:23.193
and the 18th century incest play."

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:31.730
- So thank you and
thank you for letting me

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:34.770
be a part of this even though
I'm not really a Walpoleian.

00:18:40.165 --> 00:18:43.280
A guilt ravaged woman agonizes

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:46.570
about a crime she committed 16 years ago

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:48.260
when she took the place of a maid

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:50.563
who had arranged a tryst with her son.

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:53.290
As the result of this liaison,

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:56.360
she becomes pregnant and
gives birth to a daughter.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.560
Claiming that the girl is an orphan,

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:01.300
she raises her until she at the age of 16

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:04.170
when her banished son returns home,

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:07.550
sees the beautiful girl and marries her.

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:11.080
Consternation occurs and
the woman stabs herself

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:13.860
in a fit of frenzy.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:14.963
Sound familiar?

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:19.160
What I've just described as
the plot of "The Fatal Discovery,

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:23.653
or, Love in Ruins, an anonymous
late 17th century tragedy."

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.760
Despite these similarities,
I do not mean to claim

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:29.920
that the fatal discoveries,
the source for Walpole's play

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:33.800
he claims that he got the
idea from a real-life event

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:38.220
although the advertisement
for the 1791 Dublin Edition

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:43.170
does refer to this
contemptible performance

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:46.460
as a means of praising
Walpole's greater delicacy

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:49.113
in keeping his play off the public stage.

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:53.030
Rather my interest today
lies and considering

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:56.560
"The Mysterious Mother" in the
context of three other plays

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:01.020
that like "The Fatal
Discovery" deal with incest

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.023
as the crisis point for the tragedy.

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:06.920
Unlike "The Fatal
Discovery," all three plays

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:09.130
were successful in their own time

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:11.563
and were staged well
into the 18th century.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:14.900
The first two plays I want to consider,

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:17.560
focus on brother-sister incest

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:19.290
and represent the horror it visits

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:21.720
on the unwilling participants.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.850
The first is the most
popular of all restoration

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:29.290
in 18-century incest plays,
Thomas Otways "The Orphan"

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:32.240
in which Polydore takes
his brother's place in bed

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:34.820
with Monaimia the titular orphan.

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:39.303
And here we get echoes of
orphaning from paper this morning.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:44.210
What Polydore thought was
simply an illicit assignation

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:47.990
was actually Castalio's and
Monimia's wedding night,

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:52.990
making this bed trick an act
however unwitting of incest.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:54.933
Brother with sister-in-law.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:58.480
Once discovered both
participants recognized the act

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:01.110
as a pollution that can never be removed.

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:04.180
It is a horrid deed that
results in the suicide

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:06.080
of both participants as well as

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:08.433
the sinned against, Castalio.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:12.920
A more involved but less
catastrophic example

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:16.060
occurs in Dryden's "Don Sebastian"

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:19.000
in which the hero weds his half-sister,

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:22.290
a relationship the two
discover after the fact.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:25.460
In doing so, they ignore
repeated wet warnings

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:27.540
and admonitions from other characters

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:29.840
as well as prophecies from the past

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:31.420
and in this it's reminiscent

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:36.420
of the greatest of all incest
plays Sophocles Oedipus,

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:39.980
a play that was adapted by Dryden and Lee

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:41.523
more than a decade earlier.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:46.810
Sebastian and his bride, Almeyda
consummate their marriage

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:49.150
before the relationship is known

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:52.423
but unlike the orphans bloody
conclusion, they separate.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:55.780
Almeyda to a distant convent

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.420
and Sebastian to a desert hermitage,

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:02.183
a fate similar to Walpole's
Edward and Adeliza.

00:22:04.610 --> 00:22:06.293
Walpole's two innocent sinners.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:12.730
These plays deal with
brother-sister incest

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:16.390
and while they are reminiscent
of the Edward Adeliza

00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:18.760
storyline and "The Mysterious Mother"

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.490
they have little in common
with a central focus

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.770
of Walpole's play.

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:26.453
The countess and the
act that torments her.

00:22:27.544 --> 00:22:31.030
A greater interest here is Edmund Smith's,

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:36.030
Phaedra and Hippolytus, a
play originally staged in 1707

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:39.380
with an all-star cast
including Thomas Betterton,

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:41.523
Elizabeth Barry and Ann Oldfield.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:45.020
Smith's play went
through multiple editions

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:46.330
and remained in the repertoire

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:48.079
through the end of the 18th century.

00:22:48.079 --> 00:22:51.780
Smith was a Greek scholar
and David Erskine Baker

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:55.473
describes Phaedra and
Hippolytus as a scholar's play.

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:58.500
The play has several interesting parallels

00:22:58.500 --> 00:22:59.877
to "The Mysterious Mother."

00:22:59.877 --> 00:23:01.780
The most obvious of these

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:04.970
is the nature of the incestuous desire.

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:07.783
Mother for son in this case, a stepson.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:12.640
Although Phaedra's incestuous
desires are never acted upon,

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.410
the fact that she desires Hippolytus

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:18.670
while considering him
her son is made clear.

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:22.340
She describes her feelings
as a beastial passion

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:25.890
while stressing the exact
nature of her sinful desire

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.877
and exclaiming near the
beginning of the play,

00:23:28.877 --> 00:23:29.986
"Oh can you keep it from
yourselves unknow it

00:23:29.986 --> 00:23:34.986
"or do you think I'm so gone
in guilt that I can see,

00:23:35.547 --> 00:23:38.427
"bear the looks the eyes of one who knows

00:23:38.427 --> 00:23:40.677
"my black detested crimes.

00:23:40.677 --> 00:23:43.627
"Who knows that Phaedra loves her son."

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:49.450
There is no secondary incest
in Phaedra and Hippolytus

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:52.240
and we discover early in the first act

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:55.004
the nature of Phaedra's sin.

00:23:55.004 --> 00:23:57.590
Phaedra and Hippolytus opens with Lycon,

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:59.380
a scheming Minister of State

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:02.920
who much like Walpole's
Benedict plots throughout

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:05.390
to destroy the play's heroine.

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:09.640
Smith's character however is
more successful than Benedict

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:12.870
in prompting his victim
to confess her sin

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:16.010
and under the guise of wise counsel,

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:18.940
he uses it to torment her further,

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:22.200
urging her at one point
to yield to her husband

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:26.490
Theseus's desires, advice that she regards

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:30.147
with horror exclaiming,
"In the father's arms,

00:24:30.147 --> 00:24:33.617
"Oh horrid thought, oh execrable incest.

00:24:33.617 --> 00:24:36.480
"Even in the father's arms embrace the son

00:24:37.912 --> 00:24:39.930
and this rather lurid passage was omitted

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:42.713
from the Bells edition in 1777.

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:47.060
In the end she, like the
countess, stabs herself

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:49.453
in a fit of guilt-ridden frenzy.

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:55.260
While Smith's play was
generally praised by critics,

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:58.330
some took offense with
Phaedra's willingness

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:01.220
to confess her crime in
front of an audience.

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:04.150
Seeing the event of her speech as an act

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:08.440
almost as grotesque as the
incestuous desire itself.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:13.000
Even Dryden's Norma Hall in "Aureng-zebe"

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:18.000
as Charles Gildon notes, kept
"her brutal passion to herself

00:25:18.857 --> 00:25:20.987
"not like the bolder Phaedra,

00:25:20.987 --> 00:25:24.557
"making confidants and
breathing her incestuous desires

00:25:24.557 --> 00:25:26.447
"in the face of a whole court."

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:31.140
Having incestuous desires is bad enough.

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:33.740
Articulating these desires to others

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:36.003
constitutes an even greater sin.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:41.700
Walpole himself stressed the
illogic if not the sinfulness

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:43.790
of having a character discuss her sin

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:47.590
in front of an audience.
Digressing from a discussion

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:50.583
of [indistinct] to state,

00:25:51.787 --> 00:25:53.327
"With all the difference of Grecian,

00:25:53.327 --> 00:25:57.387
"French and English manners
it is impossible to conceive

00:25:57.387 --> 00:26:00.707
"that Phaedra trusted
her incestuous passions

00:26:00.707 --> 00:26:03.727
"or Medea her murderous revenge

00:26:03.727 --> 00:26:05.947
"to a whole troupe of attendants."

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:10.410
While Walpole does not comment directly

00:26:10.410 --> 00:26:14.650
on the criminality of Phaedra
herself, his linking of her

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:17.963
with the notorious villainous
Medea is suggestive.

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:22.440
As of now I have found no definitive proof

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:25.680
that Walpole new Smith's
play but I feel certain

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:27.143
that he was familiar with it.

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:31.080
Now I do not mean by
drawing these connections

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.768
to suggest that "The Mysterious Mother"

00:26:32.768 --> 00:26:36.960
is just really a pastiche
of tropes commonly found

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:41.570
in earlier plays. Rather
examining the places

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:43.430
where it diverges from the norms

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:46.060
seen in other publicly staged plays,

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:49.900
illuminates the uniqueness
of Walpole's tragedy.

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:51.860
Uniqueness that I would argue

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:54.560
would have prevented it
from being stageable drama

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:57.340
at least in the 18th century since we know

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:58.800
from last night's performance

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:03.303
that we were able to stage it in the 21st.

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:07.830
To do so I would like to turn
back to "The Fatal Discovery"

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:10.150
that contemptible
performance with the plot

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:13.420
that so carefully parallels,
so closely parallels

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:15.093
that of "The Mysterious Mother."

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:18.370
Bad though this play may be,

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:20.840
it nevertheless possessed
qualities that allowed it

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:22.550
to appear on the stage.

00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:25.570
Something that would have been
impossible for Walpole's play

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:27.540
no matter how carefully he worked

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:30.283
to engage sympathy for his heroine.

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:35.253
The most notable differences
occur early on in the play.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:41.660
In "The Fatal Discovery,"
Beringaria the countess figure

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:45.600
does not knowingly sleep with her son

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:47.670
rather she thought she was arranging

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:51.470
to sleep with her husband
who had abandoned her bed.

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:55.670
As her confidant, Arapsia later explains

00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:59.903
the convoluted chain of
events to Baron Cornaro's son.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.890
The night before you were sent to travel,

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:06.160
you passed an engagement
with a young and foolish

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:08.820
maid of the house to meet that night.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:12.030
Your father had made
another such appointment

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.870
which was by one discovered to your mother

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:17.020
and my unhappy lady in her stead

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:20.180
resolved to meet her
husband but by accident

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:24.770
she did mistake the room and
came to you but in the morning,

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:27.860
slipping from her husband as she believed

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:30.550
for fear of discovery she met my lord

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:34.620
just come from his intrigue
and then she found too late

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:35.863
her fatal error.

00:28:37.670 --> 00:28:41.760
Like Phaedra, Beringaria
has confessed her guilt

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:44.740
to a trusted confidant but in her case,

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:48.110
the confidant taking on
the role of the playwright

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:51.300
can remind Beringaria and the audience

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:54.683
that her act was no more
than a fatal accident,

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:57.853
possibly even condoned by heaven.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:03.280
Beringaria considers the
real source of her guilt

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:06.223
to be her own wanton love for her husband.

00:29:07.190 --> 00:29:10.510
She should have considered
his desires, not her own

00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:13.910
and allowed him to continue
with his adulterous behavior.

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:17.810
Her act while not appropriately
wifely and submissive,

00:29:17.810 --> 00:29:19.980
was nothing more than an accident.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:22.693
Destructive yes but unintentional.

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:26.370
By contrast, Walpole's heroine

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:30.500
is not the victim of a fatal
error in household geography,

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:33.323
something that distinguishes
her from her predecessor.

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:37.300
Because of this Walpole explains,

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:41.070
he felt the need to palliate the crime

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:44.720
first by creating the figure
of the evil monk, Benedict

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:47.330
and then by creating a
situation in which the countess

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:51.280
was to be imagined as thrown off her guard

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:54.360
by a combination of
grief and disappointment.

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:58.790
Her fondness for her husband
was thus in some measure

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.083
the cause of her guilt.

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:04.020
If the countess's act was deliberate,

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:06.133
even if it was not premeditated.

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:09.890
She explains that in the moment
when she waited longingly

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:13.600
for her husband's return,
I mean some of these lines

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:16.223
should be familiar from
last night's performance.

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:21.770
She saw him, presented a
bloody course before me.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:22.813
"I raved.

00:30:23.717 --> 00:30:27.120
This storm of disappointed
passions assailed my reason,

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:31.340
fevered all my blood." And at that moment,

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:33.943
she learns of Edmund's
proposition to the maid.

00:30:34.913 --> 00:30:37.313
"Thou canst not harbor
a foreboding thought,

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:43.010
more dire than I conceived I executed.

00:30:43.010 --> 00:30:44.730
Guilt rushed into my soul,

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:47.513
my fancy saw thee thy father's image.

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.490
Grief, disappointment, opportunity

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:52.530
raise such a tumult in my madding blood

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:55.490
I took the damsel's place."

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:57.490
This was no accident.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:31:00.260
No case of mistaken identity.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:02.580
While the countess's raw grief born out

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:05.680
of her love for her husband
may be a mitigating factor,

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:08.350
there is no doubt she
knew what she was doing

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:12.583
even if she could not foresee
the consequences of her crime.

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:15.380
This deliberate behavior,

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:20.040
this articulated fact of
incestuous desire is what I suggest

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:22.763
would have made "The
Mysterious Mother" unstageable,

00:31:23.634 --> 00:31:25.550
a fact that while Walpole's
friend, William Mason

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.640
as we were just discussing implies

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:31.150
when he suggested a very few alterations

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:35.513
that he thought would make
the play more stageable.

00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:39.000
After praising the play he comments

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:42.130
that there is a capital
defect in the denouement

00:31:43.813 --> 00:31:46.453
and "Prove the thing feasible.

00:31:47.797 --> 00:31:50.777
"There is a sketchy
sense of such alterations

00:31:50.777 --> 00:31:53.110
"as I think necessary."

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:57.520
His advice was to render the
incestuous act an accident

00:31:57.520 --> 00:31:59.860
the result of the
countess's supposed jealousy

00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:02.580
over her husband, in other words

00:32:02.580 --> 00:32:06.170
to replicate the plot
of "The Fatal Discovery"

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:09.490
that contrived but stage worthy drama.

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:10.889
Thank you.

00:32:10.889 --> 00:32:13.139
(applause)

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:18.620
- Thank you very much Jean.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:20.760
We're gonna have lots to
talk about the stage ability

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:23.303
verses unstage ability I
can see in the talkback.

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:26.910
Next it's my pleasure
to introduce Al Coppola,

00:32:26.910 --> 00:32:30.450
who is an associate professor
of English at John Jay College

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:32.940
in the CUNY system and
he's also the past chair

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:34.970
of the Columbia University seminar

00:32:34.970 --> 00:32:37.700
in 18th century European culture.

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:39.930
His first book "The Theater of Experiment,

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:43.430
Staging Natural Philosophy
in 18th Century Britain"

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:47.530
is a critical study of
science in and as performance.

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:50.490
His current book project,
"Enlightenment Visibilities"

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:53.120
studies the 18th century
innovations that structure

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:55.860
21st century modernity by bringing

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:59.010
previously unimaginable
or imperceptible phenomena

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:01.440
into the domain of
perception and knowledge.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:04.460
Now I would like to say that
I added in the lightning

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:06.520
in last night's show just for Al

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:09.654
because he's going to be
talking to us about lightning.

00:33:09.654 --> 00:33:11.963
Let's just call it serendipity.

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:15.350
So his title for us today is

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:17.853
"Spectacles of Science and Superstition."

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:27.440
- Thank you so very
much to Cindy and to Misty

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:29.260
and to everyone that put this together

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:31.960
and for welcoming in someone who I guess

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:33.943
I can't really call myself a Walpolian.

00:33:33.943 --> 00:33:36.400
I'm actually not even
gonna be talking so much

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:38.260
about literature either today

00:33:38.260 --> 00:33:39.890
because what I want to think about is

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:42.490
sort of the scientific
contexts that are circulating

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:45.343
that I think we want to put on the table.

00:33:49.368 --> 00:33:52.920
I want us to think about
"The Mysterious Mother"

00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:55.306
for a range of reasons and certainly

00:33:55.306 --> 00:33:56.940
one of the most important
ones surely must be

00:33:56.940 --> 00:34:01.130
how this notorious drama
helped shape that emergent form

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:02.820
of the Gothic mode.

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:05.030
What do we get when we situate this play

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:08.690
alongside Walpole's
endlessly read "The Castle of Otranto"

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:12.960
as the twin headwaters of
the gothic's murky flow.

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:15.920
What was the context in the
catalyst for this play's

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:19.600
mysterious monstrous birth in 1768

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:21.570
and what can it teach us about the sources

00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:24.170
and textures and meanings of form?

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:26.490
So I'd like us to think about what happens

00:34:26.490 --> 00:34:29.090
to the supernatural in
Walpole's gothic vision

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:31.970
when we move from "Otranto"
to "The Mysterious Mother."

00:34:31.970 --> 00:34:35.300
Certainly we've got the gloomy,
moldering castle a motif

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:37.890
and there's certainly
a has an abiding sense

00:34:37.890 --> 00:34:41.460
that the places and spaces
of this story are accursed.

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:43.900
But in "the Mysterious
Mother" we find no examples

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:47.310
of the hallucinatory literalism
we had in "Otranto."

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:49.810
I'm thinking that
preposterous giant helmet

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:52.540
so mad fastly and insistently real

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:54.700
that it kills a young heir dead

00:34:54.700 --> 00:34:57.160
and even serves as a
temporary holding cell

00:34:57.160 --> 00:34:59.700
for a suspected malfactor.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:02.760
That helmet is a deliberate
and weirdly material signal

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:05.350
that Otronto is truly haunted.

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:08.620
The gothic tale is
straight-up supernatural.

00:35:08.620 --> 00:35:10.700
Past crimes of usurpation and cruelty

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:13.640
do not merely persist in guilty memory.

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:16.010
Those wrongs are real and the very palace

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:19.410
is haunted by supernatural
agents that avenge them

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:21.150
in fulfillment of what it seemed like

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:23.710
a cryptically figurative prophecy.

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:26.960
Turns out the warning, the
castle on lordship of Otranto

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:28.910
shall pass from the present family

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:31.010
whenever the real owner
should be grown too large

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:34.530
to inhabit it, that was absurdly literal.

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:37.590
Cue the supernatural, exit
Manfred running from a giant

00:35:38.645 --> 00:35:41.260
but "The Mysterious Mother"
is quite different.

00:35:41.260 --> 00:35:43.860
In fact it posits a
deliberately anti supernatural

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:45.180
version of the Gothic.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.090
No Giants stalk these ancient halls,

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:49.400
no skeletons rattle their bones,

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:51.220
talking portraits are right out.

00:35:51.220 --> 00:35:54.110
The castle and heirs of
Narbonne are cursed for sure

00:35:54.110 --> 00:35:55.090
but there's no evidence

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:57.300
that supernatural agents are to blame.

00:35:57.300 --> 00:35:59.770
In fact the thunderstorm
that we did have to cut

00:35:59.770 --> 00:36:03.610
to make it stageable plays
such an important role

00:36:03.610 --> 00:36:05.440
in act two, scene two

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:08.130
that so terrifies the
denizens of the place.

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:09.850
It triggers a pitched battle

00:36:09.850 --> 00:36:12.890
about the very possibility
of supernatural agency

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:14.870
and it becomes an opportunity for Walpole

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.643
to explicitly disavow it.

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:21.030
So we begin with Florian and Edmund

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:22.880
meeting the procession
of orphaned children

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:25.270
making a pilgrimage to the count's grave

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:27.400
on the anniversary of his sudden death.

00:36:27.400 --> 00:36:29.740
When Florian overhears
the scheming priest Martin

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:32.580
encourage the children's
superstitious belief

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:36.240
that the count is haunting the shrine,

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:38.413
the blunt-talking soldier confronts him.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.420
His friend Edmund might
kneel to omnipotence,

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:45.122
Florian says but that does not mean

00:36:45.122 --> 00:36:48.817
"Each gossips dream, each
village fable domineers

00:36:48.817 --> 00:36:52.190
"in turn his brains distempered nerves."

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:55.060
And it's at this point
that the storm comes on.

00:36:55.060 --> 00:36:57.430
Walpole stage directions read,

00:36:57.430 --> 00:36:59.920
"violent storm of thunder and lightning."

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:03.580
But this triggers a debate
about its proper interpretation.

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:06.797
Martin declaims, "Will
this convince thee.

00:37:06.797 --> 00:37:09.507
"Where's the gossips dream,
the village fable now?

00:37:09.507 --> 00:37:12.663
"Hear Heaven's own voice
condemn impiety,"

00:37:14.145 --> 00:37:16.747
and Florian giving as good
as he gets respond back,

00:37:16.747 --> 00:37:19.723
"Hear Heaven's own voice
condemn imposture."

00:37:20.867 --> 00:37:22.320
"What says the storm?"

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:24.900
asked Florian in the scene quite pointedly.

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:27.890
And I'd say that Walpole is
asking it to attend very closely

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:28.783
to this question.

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:31.827
"Pronounces it for thee or me?"

00:37:31.827 --> 00:37:35.317
Asks "do none dispute
within the compass of its bolt

00:37:35.317 --> 00:37:37.857
but we? Is the same loud-voiced oracle

00:37:37.857 --> 00:37:41.747
"definitive for fifty various brawls?

00:37:41.747 --> 00:37:44.540
"Or but a shock of clouds to all but us?"

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:47.280
In fact Walpole has
placed in Florian's mouth

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:50.150
a compelling argument
against superstitious belief

00:37:50.150 --> 00:37:53.740
and the credulous or knavish narcissism

00:37:53.740 --> 00:37:56.070
that would feed it and would assume that

00:37:56.070 --> 00:38:00.330
some storm is some sign from God.

00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:02.240
Now it's a compelling argument but I'd add

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:05.800
we need to remember it's a
very very timely argument

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:08.190
for the 1760s were the decade that saw

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:10.660
an intense interest in
the nature of electricity

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:11.820
and in particular,

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.250
its relationship to lightning and thunder.

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:16.870
So Benjamin Franklin of
course published experiments

00:38:16.870 --> 00:38:20.010
and observations on electricity in 1751

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:21.810
which theorized that the electrical fluid

00:38:21.810 --> 00:38:24.250
that natural philosophers
had been experimenting with

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:27.480
via electrostatic
generators and Leyden jars

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:29.100
was the same phenomena responsible

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:32.093
for the awesome destructive
power of electrical storms.

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:35.640
Many Naturals accepted
Franklin's hypothesis

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:37.224
but even into the 1760s,

00:38:37.224 --> 00:38:40.490
more than a few prominent dissenters

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:43.360
resisted Franklin's claims
in scientific circles

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:45.750
following the lead of the
Abbé Nollet who spoke out

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:49.170
against identifying
lightning with electricity.

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:52.850
However the 1760s drew
the curtain on what became

00:38:52.850 --> 00:38:55.490
the second act of this
scientific controversy.

00:38:55.490 --> 00:38:57.910
While most conceded
lightning was electricity,

00:38:57.910 --> 00:39:01.410
there was a decades-long
dispute that was erupted here

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:03.710
about the efficacy of lightning rods.

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:07.450
Perhaps what was the defining
scientific matter of concern

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:09.650
in the later 18th
century and in this sense

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:11.160
I'm thinking about Bruno Latour's

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:13.090
thinking about matters of concern

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:15.896
as in cautious distinction
of matters of fact.

00:39:15.896 --> 00:39:19.410
Since 1752, Franklin had been advocating

00:39:19.410 --> 00:39:21.590
and installing pointed lightning rods

00:39:21.590 --> 00:39:24.430
to draw down electrical
charges from threatening clouds

00:39:24.430 --> 00:39:26.840
in order to lower the
likelihood and decrease

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:28.920
the deadliness of lightning strikes.

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:31.290
Now many English
naturalist remain skeptical

00:39:31.290 --> 00:39:33.630
of their efficacy at all while others

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:35.600
through their support
behind Benjamin Watson

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:38.790
who advocated round-tip
lightning rods and in fact

00:39:38.790 --> 00:39:40.420
the first lightning
rods were only installed

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:43.980
in England in 1762 just two years later

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:47.693
and just two years later an
article by Benjamin Wilson

00:39:47.693 --> 00:39:49.450
alleged that they would in
fact prove more dangerous

00:39:49.450 --> 00:39:51.693
by making lightning strikes more likely.

00:39:52.850 --> 00:39:55.010
So while this philosophical dispute raged

00:39:55.010 --> 00:39:56.780
in the learned press and in the pages

00:39:56.780 --> 00:40:00.100
of the Philosophical Transactions,
the newspapers of the day

00:40:00.100 --> 00:40:02.010
devoted a remarkable degree of attention

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:04.820
to reports of lightning
and electrical storms.

00:40:04.820 --> 00:40:09.260
The 16th of August 1764 edition
of the "St. James Chronicle"

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:12.707
reported "A severe storm
of thunder and lightning

00:40:12.707 --> 00:40:15.430
"recently experienced in Boston,"

00:40:15.430 --> 00:40:18.340
"the heavens appeared
in one continual blaze

00:40:18.340 --> 00:40:21.510
as each rooftop lightning
called down the celestial fire

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:24.340
in a what appeared to be a
dazzling array of patterns"

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:26.190
to the eyewitness.

00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:29.180
Just one mighty strike
did damage it was reported

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:30.810
but there were no points erected

00:40:30.810 --> 00:40:32.590
within a half mile of this house

00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:34.710
and so as you can infer this

00:40:34.710 --> 00:40:36.230
and so many of the other reports

00:40:36.230 --> 00:40:39.880
to appear in the popular
press and "Gentleman's Magazine"

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:41.980
during these years explicitly advocated

00:40:41.980 --> 00:40:44.320
for the erection of lightning rods

00:40:44.320 --> 00:40:46.940
but that's not to say they all did.

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:51.670
The 11th of April 1765
"Gazetteer and New Advertiser

00:40:51.670 --> 00:40:54.240
published an account from
a maritime correspondent

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:57.077
who reported that, "During a violent storm

00:40:57.077 --> 00:40:58.747
"of thunder lightning and wind,

00:40:58.747 --> 00:41:02.137
"a luminous body surrounded
the spindle at their masthead

00:41:02.137 --> 00:41:05.417
"which appeared to be
a perfect body of fire.

00:41:05.417 --> 00:41:08.147
"May it not be conjectured,"
that's the editor,

00:41:08.147 --> 00:41:10.227
"that aren't spindles at
the mastheads of ships

00:41:10.227 --> 00:41:13.640
"often attract the lightning
that would otherwise escape."

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:15.420
Yet that same newspaper a few months later

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:19.200
published a harrowing account
penned by Thomas Erskine

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:21.920
who is the master of the
Royal Navy frigate Tartar

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:23.910
about an electrical storm
that he went through

00:41:23.910 --> 00:41:25.720
off the coast of Florida.

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:27.830
After first having been
struck by lightning

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:30.500
on the quarterdeck,
Erskine was brought below

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:32.010
for medical attention.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:36.407
However, as he wrote, he,
"Had not been down a minute

00:41:36.407 --> 00:41:39.217
"when we heard a noise equal
to all the guns of the ship

00:41:39.217 --> 00:41:40.527
"exploding at once.

00:41:40.527 --> 00:41:42.887
"The shock of lightning or electricity

00:41:42.887 --> 00:41:46.404
"destroyed in an instant
the mainmast, main topmast,

00:41:46.404 --> 00:41:49.477
"main top gallant mast, tearing
them in a million pieces,

00:41:49.477 --> 00:41:51.397
"large splinters flying
all around the ship

00:41:51.397 --> 00:41:52.687
"from many yards distant.

00:41:52.687 --> 00:41:55.297
"The sails rent in as
many pieces or threads,

00:41:55.297 --> 00:41:58.257
"streams of electric fire
rushing at the same time

00:41:58.257 --> 00:41:59.867
"down to the bottom of the ship.

00:41:59.867 --> 00:42:02.897
"In short never was
anything more tremendous."

00:42:04.050 --> 00:42:05.543
I think this helps contextualize

00:42:05.543 --> 00:42:08.540
the way Walpole concludes his storm scene.

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:10.410
The children come running back on stage

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:12.810
interrupting the men's
debate by carrying news

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:15.427
of an offstage conflagration.

00:42:15.427 --> 00:42:17.817
"Oh a storm so dreadful,"
cries one orphan.

00:42:17.817 --> 00:42:20.380
"Some demon rides in the air."

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:22.140
Martin cynically goading them on

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:24.493
asked if none had the
courage to face the fiend

00:42:24.493 --> 00:42:27.697
and one reports, "I winked
and I saw the lightning

00:42:27.697 --> 00:42:28.997
"burst the monument.

00:42:28.997 --> 00:42:31.317
"The shield of arms shivered to splinters.

00:42:31.317 --> 00:42:34.127
"Ere I could repeat an Ave Maria
down with a hideous crash

00:42:34.127 --> 00:42:35.877
"the cross came tumbling."

00:42:36.850 --> 00:42:39.450
Walpole didn't have to
embellish this scene.

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:41.600
We know that one of the
reasons why lightning rods

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:43.950
were such a crucial matter of concern,

00:42:43.950 --> 00:42:46.960
a genuine article of debate is that

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:48.640
they drew down as it were,

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:50.900
lingering superstitions about the causes

00:42:50.900 --> 00:42:53.960
of sublime natural events
like lightning storms

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:56.960
while the question of the efficacy

00:42:56.960 --> 00:42:58.860
and the proper design of lightning rods

00:42:58.860 --> 00:43:00.650
was being debated by the learned

00:43:00.650 --> 00:43:02.480
on both sides of the Atlantic,

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:04.610
people were still being
electrocuted to death

00:43:04.610 --> 00:43:06.240
in shockingly high numbers

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:08.810
due to the persistence
of pre-modern beliefs

00:43:08.810 --> 00:43:10.340
that lightning was an act of God

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:12.523
and a sign of his displeasure.

00:43:12.523 --> 00:43:17.523
And as Trent Mitchell discusses
in a really great article

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:20.913
in 18th-century studies in 1998,

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:26.650
according to many contemporaries
the only true safeguards

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:29.910
from this providential instrument
were a full trust in God

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:33.230
and a hearty repentance to
move God's heavy judgment.

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:36.060
So clergy not unlike the
clergy we see in the play

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:38.060
might be using instances of lightning

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:40.150
to extend their moral
influence and authority

00:43:40.150 --> 00:43:42.490
over the flock, as Martin does

00:43:42.490 --> 00:43:44.350
but it also meant they
actually have to do something

00:43:44.350 --> 00:43:45.580
when storm's threatened.

00:43:45.580 --> 00:43:48.540
And so generally this meant
the ringing of church bells

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:50.970
but of course this exposed the faithful

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:53.110
to terrible deaths by electrocution

00:43:53.110 --> 00:43:56.250
if they were up in the Campanile
yanking on wet bell pulls

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:58.040
when lightning struck.

00:43:58.040 --> 00:44:03.040
Now this is not some gothic
prehistoric superstition.

00:44:03.340 --> 00:44:07.270
One report issued in Munich
found that over a 33 year period

00:44:07.270 --> 00:44:10.560
386 lightning strikes hit bell towers

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:14.730
and they resulted in 103 fatalities.

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:18.060
This report was published in 1784.

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:21.198
No wonder that the "Lloyds
Evening Post" of May 1768,

00:44:21.198 --> 00:44:24.320
the same year Walpole penned
"The Mysterious Mother,"

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:26.060
felt the need to publish a long extract

00:44:26.060 --> 00:44:28.400
from the history of the
Royal Academy of Paris

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:31.370
on how to "Preserve
one's self from Thunder."

00:44:31.370 --> 00:44:35.320
TLDR don't hunt under
trees and for God's sake

00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:37.430
don't ring any bells. And I think

00:44:38.860 --> 00:44:40.920
there are a number of plausible reasons

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:43.750
why Walpole's version of the
Gothic shifts in this way

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:47.090
from "The Castle of Otranto"
to "The Mysterious Mother."

00:44:47.090 --> 00:44:48.470
Chief among them I think is

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:50.220
as we've been discussing Walpole's

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:52.430
obstinate retention of that incest plot

00:44:52.430 --> 00:44:55.720
and his insistence that this
play must be at a heart,

00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:58.000
investigation how the countess copes

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:01.080
with the unimaginable guilt of an act

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:02.933
of a truly diseased imagination.

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:06.900
He wants to focus it
on internal struggles.

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:10.350
Too much attention paid
to supernatural agents

00:45:10.350 --> 00:45:13.600
would distract from his
analysis of morbid psychology.

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:15.390
Besides Walpole may have quite sensibly

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:17.690
been writing against the
formal material constraints

00:45:17.690 --> 00:45:18.610
of the theater.

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:20.150
It is a bit of an open question

00:45:20.150 --> 00:45:21.890
whether he really thought
this could be staged

00:45:21.890 --> 00:45:23.640
but I'm convinced it for a while there

00:45:23.640 --> 00:45:26.630
they thought this might
have might have worked.

00:45:26.630 --> 00:45:28.280
One can certainly more readily narrate

00:45:28.280 --> 00:45:30.110
an electrical storm offstage

00:45:30.110 --> 00:45:33.563
than one can represent a
giant falling cask on stage.

00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:36.550
But I do think the play's
curious treatment of electricity

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:39.470
in particular is
deliberate for it registers

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:42.420
and draws cultural force from
the ambiguity and suspicion

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:44.060
that gathers around lightning

00:45:44.060 --> 00:45:46.600
as this kind of crucial matter of concern.

00:45:46.600 --> 00:45:47.740
These electrical debates

00:45:47.740 --> 00:45:50.800
revealed a certain
persistence of pre enlightened

00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:54.260
gothic credulity and while
Walpole's protagonists

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:57.130
reject Martin's supernatural
trappings outright

00:45:57.130 --> 00:45:58.690
And I think they speak in the voice

00:45:58.690 --> 00:46:01.000
we're supposed to
acknowledge and agree with,

00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:03.090
the very popularity of this Gothic mode

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:05.450
that Walpole initiates exploits

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:08.890
even as it exercises these
pre-modern fears and longings

00:46:08.890 --> 00:46:12.010
and it gives voice and
form to a wider suspicion

00:46:12.010 --> 00:46:14.870
of the enlightenment project
and discourses of science

00:46:14.870 --> 00:46:18.440
And it's that wider suspicion
of the discourses of science

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:19.350
and the enlightenment project

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:23.040
that I think is in the air in 1768.

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:24.670
That's the same year as Joseph Wright's

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:28.530
remarkable portrait experiment
on a bird in an air-pump

00:46:28.530 --> 00:46:29.968
and if you're familiar with that,

00:46:29.968 --> 00:46:34.968
the center of the portrait
is that the sphinx-like gaze

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:39.960
of Wright's scientist who sort
of stares blankly at us

00:46:39.960 --> 00:46:44.760
with his hand on the
stopcock of that air-pump

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:47.650
about to let the air back in
to bring that bird back to life

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:49.390
but were caught in that
split-second moment

00:46:49.390 --> 00:46:51.760
of anticipation and fear.

00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:53.490
I feel like "The Mysterious Mother"

00:46:53.490 --> 00:46:56.660
as much as his sphinx-like
gaze seems to be asking us

00:46:57.680 --> 00:47:00.980
we're moderns aren't we or
if we've never been modern

00:47:00.980 --> 00:47:04.580
or we aren't modern yet or we
can't ever hope to be modern,

00:47:04.580 --> 00:47:05.580
what a thrill it is

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:07.633
to be caught in the play of these forces.

00:47:08.747 --> 00:47:09.680
Thank you.

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:11.930
(applause)

00:47:15.877 --> 00:47:16.710
- Thank you so much.

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:17.543
Fascinating.

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:20.590
All right moving right along.

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:23.790
Our next paper will
come from Judith Hawley

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:26.210
who is a professor of
18th century literature

00:47:26.210 --> 00:47:28.170
in the Department of
English Royal Holloway,

00:47:28.170 --> 00:47:29.720
University of London.

00:47:29.720 --> 00:47:31.300
Her research interests include

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:35.260
the history of amateur
performance, hooray,

00:47:35.260 --> 00:47:38.320
as well as publishing on
Laurence Sterne, encyclopedias,

00:47:38.320 --> 00:47:41.150
Siamese twins and Grub Street,

00:47:41.150 --> 00:47:44.040
she has audited various
18th century texts.

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:47.420
Currently she's writing a group biography

00:47:47.420 --> 00:47:49.727
of Pope Swift and the Scribblers Club.

00:47:49.727 --> 00:47:52.080
She's a regular speaker on the radio,

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:55.750
most recently appearing on a
discussion of the gin craze

00:47:55.750 --> 00:47:58.423
in "On Our Time" BBC Radio four.

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:01.280
Her title for us today

00:48:01.280 --> 00:48:04.740
is "The Beautiful
Negligence of a Gentleman,

00:48:04.740 --> 00:48:07.313
Horace Walpole and Amateur Theatricals."

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:13.000
- Thank you very much Misty.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:17.810
I'm very happy to add to
this wonderful thank fest

00:48:17.810 --> 00:48:18.820
we've been having here.

00:48:18.820 --> 00:48:21.600
I don't want to name people
what I really want to thank

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:24.150
everybody who's been involved
in making this thing happen.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:26.363
it's been oops, it disappeared.

00:48:28.260 --> 00:48:29.970
I shouldn't try to do two things at once.

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:31.715
It's there, it's good.

00:48:31.715 --> 00:48:35.590
Also a little bit of a
modesty trooper Walpolian

00:48:35.590 --> 00:48:38.450
modesty troop which means
it's not really modest at all

00:48:38.450 --> 00:48:41.901
but I will be using some of the
same examples and quotations

00:48:41.901 --> 00:48:43.500
that you have heard already

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:48.496
and I'll try to cut a few as I go

00:48:48.496 --> 00:48:49.940
but I don't want to mess up the paper

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:53.880
in the act of trying not to say something

00:48:53.880 --> 00:48:55.770
that you've heard from
one of our other speakers.

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:57.730
Anyway, here we go.

00:48:57.730 --> 00:48:59.120
It's generally assumed

00:48:59.120 --> 00:49:01.910
that it was because of its
sensational subject matter

00:49:01.910 --> 00:49:03.490
that Walpole's Mysterious Mother

00:49:03.490 --> 00:49:05.480
did not have a public
performance in his day

00:49:05.480 --> 00:49:07.553
but I think the situation
is more complex than that.

00:49:07.553 --> 00:49:10.480
It's not just the incest plot.

00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:12.970
Both Walpole's feelings
about professional theater

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:16.200
and public reactions to his
play were not clear-cut.

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:19.500
For example the "Monthly Review"
considered it no less proper

00:49:19.500 --> 00:49:22.330
for representation than Otways "Orphan"

00:49:22.330 --> 00:49:24.500
which included a triple suicide.

00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:27.550
Moreover David Worrall has
discovered that it was adapted

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:31.420
and performed at Thomas
Dibden's in Surrey theater

00:49:31.420 --> 00:49:35.630
admittedly not till 1821,
thus after Walpole's death.

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:39.680
He calculates it must have been
seen by about 16,000 people

00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:42.730
who didn't write, didn't
complain to the newspapers

00:49:42.730 --> 00:49:45.124
or otherwise signal their disappropriation

00:49:45.124 --> 00:49:47.290
of public dramatization in central London

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:50.683
of one of society's most
supposedly sequestered vices.

00:49:51.860 --> 00:49:54.820
Now the context in 1821 as David

00:49:54.820 --> 00:49:58.440
definitely demonstrates is very
different from that of 1768.

00:49:58.440 --> 00:50:01.840
But nonetheless I do think
that Walpole at some point

00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:03.410
and in some part of himself

00:50:03.410 --> 00:50:07.070
did envisage a public performance
and wrote a comic epilogue

00:50:07.070 --> 00:50:10.220
for Kitty Clive to read
which we've heard

00:50:10.220 --> 00:50:11.867
but he did say, "But I'm not so sure

00:50:11.867 --> 00:50:13.557
"that she'd like to speak it."

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:18.150
But the responses of his
even his close circle

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:20.910
to this story of illicit
passion quickly made him realize

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:22.550
that for the sake of his reputation,

00:50:22.550 --> 00:50:24.100
he must suppress the play.

00:50:24.100 --> 00:50:26.458
Within weeks of completing it, Walpole

00:50:26.458 --> 00:50:29.040
admitted to his old school
friend George Montagu

00:50:29.040 --> 00:50:33.200
in April 1768, he did not
think it would do for the stage.

00:50:33.200 --> 00:50:35.530
And I'm going to come
back to that quotation

00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:38.800
because I think that
idea of what he means by

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:41.473
would it do for the stages
is a little bit tricky.

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:44.330
So I want to consider why Walpole

00:50:44.330 --> 00:50:46.180
did not think it would do for the stage

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:48.520
and what that might tell
us about theatrical culture

00:50:48.520 --> 00:50:50.640
in the second half of the 18th century.

00:50:50.640 --> 00:50:54.360
I'm going to focus on his
aesthetic and social motivation

00:50:54.360 --> 00:50:56.170
more than the psychosexual ones.

00:50:56.170 --> 00:50:58.640
Other critics have read the plot of incest

00:50:58.640 --> 00:51:00.870
and maternal desire in
"The Mysterious Mother," as

00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:04.420
Walpole's way of both representing

00:51:04.420 --> 00:51:07.083
and concealing homosexual feelings.

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:11.400
Jill Campbell for example has demonstrated

00:51:12.920 --> 00:51:15.650
very deftly how that can be done

00:51:15.650 --> 00:51:18.640
and how one needs to be
careful about doing it.

00:51:19.730 --> 00:51:21.310
But I'm going to consider
"The Mysterious Mother"

00:51:21.310 --> 00:51:23.270
from it the theater history point of view

00:51:23.270 --> 00:51:24.940
and also to consider theater history

00:51:24.940 --> 00:51:26.870
from a different vantage point.

00:51:26.870 --> 00:51:28.760
"The Mysterious Mother" was never performed

00:51:28.760 --> 00:51:32.300
in London's patent theaters
but performance in Drury Lane,

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:35.330
Covent Garden, Haymarket was
not the only option available

00:51:35.330 --> 00:51:37.153
to playwrights in the 18th century.

00:51:38.330 --> 00:51:39.970
So if it wouldn't do for the stage,

00:51:39.970 --> 00:51:42.520
what kind of venue was it suited to?

00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:44.870
What kind of work does
Walpole think it was

00:51:44.870 --> 00:51:46.830
and what might that tell
us about theatrical culture

00:51:46.830 --> 00:51:49.510
in the second half of the 18th century?

00:51:49.510 --> 00:51:50.940
Is it a closet drama?

00:51:50.940 --> 00:51:53.573
Now we've heard something
about this already today.

00:51:54.720 --> 00:51:57.030
Closet dramas written to be read silently

00:51:57.030 --> 00:51:59.180
or out loud in small
groups were fairly common

00:51:59.180 --> 00:52:00.400
in the early modern period

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:03.840
and enjoyed increasing
popularity in the Romantic era

00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:06.410
when the taste for melodrama,
pantomime and comedy

00:52:06.410 --> 00:52:08.733
were driving serious verse
drama from the public stage

00:52:08.733 --> 00:52:10.603
or that was the perception.

00:52:12.318 --> 00:52:15.860
Walpole's play predates Joanna Baillie's

00:52:15.860 --> 00:52:19.190
great revival of the closet
drama by about 30 years

00:52:19.190 --> 00:52:23.600
and he's such a a pioneer in
inventing the gothic novel,

00:52:23.600 --> 00:52:25.080
inventing the gothic play.

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:28.403
Is he also inventing a new
form of closet theater.

00:52:29.450 --> 00:52:32.740
Get a sense of what early
modern closet drama was like

00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:38.840
and why it might appeal to Walpole.

00:52:38.840 --> 00:52:41.390
I'm going to quote Marta Strazynski

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:43.930
who says that in the early modern period

00:52:43.930 --> 00:52:45.380
it was more common among communities

00:52:45.380 --> 00:52:48.020
which felt isolated
from mainstream culture.

00:52:48.020 --> 00:52:51.630
She describes the former's part
of a larger cultural matrix

00:52:51.630 --> 00:52:55.000
in which closed spaces, selective
interpretive communities

00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:58.120
and political dissent are aligned.

00:52:58.120 --> 00:53:02.050
It is notoriously difficult
to define closet drama.

00:53:02.050 --> 00:53:03.950
Jacqueline Mulholland
points out quite recently

00:53:03.950 --> 00:53:05.390
that the term has a range of meanings

00:53:05.390 --> 00:53:07.480
from written to be read rather

00:53:07.480 --> 00:53:10.020
than performed to unperformable

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:12.300
either because not meeting the aesthetic

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:13.370
or moral standards of the time

00:53:13.370 --> 00:53:17.133
or simply being too bad to be performed.

00:53:18.470 --> 00:53:20.610
Catherine Burroughs has
challenged the firmness

00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:22.540
of the distinction
between closet and stage

00:53:22.540 --> 00:53:24.480
especially if that distinction is figured

00:53:24.480 --> 00:53:26.770
in terms of a binary
opposition between the closet

00:53:26.770 --> 00:53:30.960
as private, domestic,
literary, mental and feminine

00:53:30.960 --> 00:53:34.390
and the theater as public,
spectacular, performative,

00:53:34.390 --> 00:53:36.120
physical and masculine.

00:53:36.120 --> 00:53:40.170
Walpole is nothing if not
a straddler of borders.

00:53:40.170 --> 00:53:41.240
The first outing of the play

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:45.020
was a kind of closeted
performance however defined.

00:53:45.020 --> 00:53:48.420
On the 17th of April
1768 Walpole and Conway

00:53:48.420 --> 00:53:51.210
read the play to an audience
comprising Lady Aylesbury,

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:53.230
Lady Littleton and Miss Rich.

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:56.930
And I don't think their
responses to that are recorded

00:53:56.930 --> 00:54:00.903
but it would be so nice
to hear what they said.

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:03.890
We know that he had 50 copies of the play

00:54:03.890 --> 00:54:07.090
printed at Strawberry Hill
press for private circulation

00:54:07.090 --> 00:54:09.830
and that some of these books went astray,

00:54:09.830 --> 00:54:12.045
were circulated in manuscripts

00:54:12.045 --> 00:54:16.070
and that he couldn't control
what people did with his play

00:54:16.070 --> 00:54:17.530
or where it went.

00:54:17.530 --> 00:54:19.690
About 18 months after it was printed,

00:54:19.690 --> 00:54:22.200
Walpole learned that Montagu
and a group of male friends,

00:54:22.200 --> 00:54:25.430
the boys, had found his
play at Montagu's house

00:54:25.430 --> 00:54:27.710
and insisted on leaving
off gambling and drinking

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:28.940
to read it through together.

00:54:28.940 --> 00:54:30.810
He was not pleased.

00:54:30.810 --> 00:54:33.097
I beg you would keep
it under lock and key.

00:54:33.097 --> 00:54:35.123
It is not at all food for the public.

00:54:36.180 --> 00:54:37.590
So even Walpole thought

00:54:37.590 --> 00:54:40.470
this limited kind of public
performance would not do

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:42.120
and seem to think that
the privileged owners

00:54:42.120 --> 00:54:43.770
should keep it under lock and key.
1128
00:54:45,123 --&gt; 00:54:49,960
And he restricted access to
even some of his close friends

00:54:49.960 --> 00:54:52.350
who thought that they should
have had a copy sooner.

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:55.410
Horace Mann didn't see
a copy of it until 1779

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:57.620
when his nephew brought
it out to him in Florence

00:54:57.620 --> 00:55:01.960
and the nephew are supposedly
learnt it by heart on the way.

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:03.482
Mann was delighted with it

00:55:03.482 --> 00:55:05.210
and thought it perfect
for closeted performance

00:55:05.210 --> 00:55:08.760
as his nephew proved by
performing an impromptu iteration

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:10.450
and what Paul Baines has described

00:55:10.450 --> 00:55:12.393
as a pleasantly gothic setting.

00:55:13.510 --> 00:55:16.360
I won't read it but I'll
move on just to mention also

00:55:16.360 --> 00:55:20.670
a performance that we've
several times discussed

00:55:21.986 --> 00:55:23.530
over the course of the day.

00:55:23.530 --> 00:55:27.710
The wonderful sort of
delicious illicit performance

00:55:27.710 --> 00:55:30.420
that occurred in the palace in 1786

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:32.430
when Frances Burney chanced upon the copy

00:55:32.430 --> 00:55:34.220
that Lord Harcourt had.

00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:36.880
Lord Harcourt lent his
copy to Queen Caroline,

00:55:36.880 --> 00:55:39.177
she saw it and said, "Oh
this is by my friend Horace.

00:55:39.177 --> 00:55:40.350
"I think we'll read this aloud,"

00:55:40.350 --> 00:55:43.180
invited people around and
this little growing horror

00:55:43.180 --> 00:55:46.193
when they realized quite
what they were reading.

00:55:47.690 --> 00:55:51.880
Nonetheless Walpole did
not completely rule out

00:55:51.880 --> 00:55:55.160
public performance. In the
1768 letter to Montagu

00:55:55.160 --> 00:55:57.120
which I quoted earlier after admitting

00:55:57.120 --> 00:56:00.010
that he did not think it
would do for the stage,

00:56:00.010 --> 00:56:02.707
he added, "Though I wish to see acted."

00:56:04.070 --> 00:56:07.750
In 1769 the letter to Montagu
is carefully balanced.

00:56:07.750 --> 00:56:10.230
"It is not at all food for the public,

00:56:10.230 --> 00:56:14.340
at least not until I am
food for worms, good Percy"

00:56:14.340 --> 00:56:17.180
alluding to Shakespeare.

00:56:17.180 --> 00:56:19.170
Thus he does leave open the possibility

00:56:19.170 --> 00:56:22.440
of a posthumous performance.
And he does seem

00:56:22.440 --> 00:56:24.050
to have consulted his friend William Mason

00:56:24.050 --> 00:56:25.806
on how to prepare it for the stage.

00:56:25.806 --> 00:56:29.869
Some of us looked at Mason's
mark top copy earlier today

00:56:29.869 --> 00:56:31.848
and one thing that should be said is that

00:56:31.848 --> 00:56:36.460
Mason and Walpole seem
to be an exchanging plays

00:56:36.460 --> 00:56:40.640
backwards and forwards, asking
each other to advise them.

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:45.640
So Mason's advice on how
to edit and make acceptable

00:56:46.210 --> 00:56:49.127
Mysterious Mother comes
immediately after Walpole

00:56:49.127 --> 00:56:51.550
had just written to him about two comedies

00:56:51.550 --> 00:56:55.240
Mason had sent him and Walpole told Mason

00:56:55.240 --> 00:56:58.350
one of them's good yeah,
we should get this put on

00:56:58.350 --> 00:56:59.620
but do it anonymously.

00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:01.270
The other one not so good.

00:57:02.204 --> 00:57:05.210
So it's part of this sort
of economy of circulation

00:57:05.210 --> 00:57:07.620
and advising and we'll
Walpole was quite involved

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:11.990
with dealing with the
professionals in the theater

00:57:11.990 --> 00:57:15.410
and getting things put
on and helping Jephson

00:57:15.410 --> 00:57:17.300
is someone we've already
heard about as well.

00:57:17.300 --> 00:57:18.500
I'll come back to him.

00:57:18.500 --> 00:57:21.930
Lots of advice backwards and
forwards about revisions,

00:57:21.930 --> 00:57:25.173
dealing with theater managers.

00:57:28.970 --> 00:57:31.630
Publicly he thanked Mason.

00:57:31.630 --> 00:57:33.277
He said, "Wonderful you're a genius.

00:57:33.277 --> 00:57:34.500
"I don't know how you did it,"

00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:37.510
but privately Walpole
rejected Mason's suggestions

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:40.440
because he felt they
totally destroyed his object

00:57:40.440 --> 00:57:43.520
which was to exhibit a character
who's sincere penitence

00:57:43.520 --> 00:57:46.460
was not degraded by superstitious bigotry

00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:48.360
as he wrote on the back of the letter.

00:57:49.330 --> 00:57:51.850
He thanked him profusely for it

00:57:51.850 --> 00:57:54.400
but taking such pains with this play for

00:57:54.400 --> 00:57:58.193
as Mr. Gray could tell you
I cannot correct myself.

00:57:59.870 --> 00:58:02.697
Walpole's attitude to correction
is connected I suggest

00:58:02.697 --> 00:58:06.820
to his attitude to the
possibilities for public performance

00:58:06.820 --> 00:58:10.020
and we can see this more clearly
in another letter to Mason.

00:58:10.020 --> 00:58:13.020
This time about works produced
at the Strawberry Hill Press.

00:58:15.280 --> 00:58:19.127
He defiantly declared, "I
hope future edition mongers

00:58:19.127 --> 00:58:20.967
"will say of those at Strawberry Hill,

00:58:20.967 --> 00:58:23.990
"they have all the beautiful
negligence of a gentleman."

00:58:23.990 --> 00:58:26.200
He liked the typos.

00:58:26.200 --> 00:58:28.313
He didn't want them corrected.

00:58:29.630 --> 00:58:31.890
As a scholar and an editor,
my first thought is that

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:35.880
Walpole is boasting of a
kind of culpable carelessness

00:58:35.880 --> 00:58:39.700
and neglect a lack of
regard for what is correct.

00:58:39.700 --> 00:58:42.000
But I think he has in
mind the original meaning

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:44.237
of the term negligence.

00:58:44.237 --> 00:58:47.900
Careless indifference as
in appearance or dress

00:58:47.900 --> 00:58:50.410
or in literary or artistic style.

00:58:50.410 --> 00:58:53.980
Later freedom from
artificiality or restraint

00:58:53.980 --> 00:58:56.590
also an instance of this now rare.

00:58:56.590 --> 00:59:00.990
So it's freedom that he's after.

00:59:00.990 --> 00:59:04.130
Freedom to experiment originality

00:59:04.130 --> 00:59:09.130
that he's praising in
himself. But specifically

00:59:09.140 --> 00:59:12.090
an indifference to professional standards,

00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:15.810
an indifference which grants
him freedom from restraint.

00:59:15.810 --> 00:59:20.320
Crucially it's a freedom
granted him by his class status.

00:59:20.320 --> 00:59:21.700
Whether discussing the moral character

00:59:21.700 --> 00:59:23.300
of the countess in his play

00:59:23.300 --> 00:59:25.927
or the correct printing of
characters at [indistinct],

00:59:28.930 --> 00:59:32.330
Walpole expresses an aristocratic disdain

00:59:32.330 --> 00:59:35.530
for the vulgarity of professionals.

00:59:35.530 --> 00:59:38.479
In the 1769 letter to
Montagu I quoted earlier,

00:59:38.479 --> 00:59:40.760
when Walpole argue that
he did not want his play

00:59:40.760 --> 00:59:42.210
performed during his lifetime,

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:44.540
he didn't mention his moral reputation

00:59:44.540 --> 00:59:47.100
rather he complained about
the current standards

00:59:47.100 --> 00:59:48.943
of the professional theater.

00:59:51.700 --> 00:59:53.790
"Nay, it is not an age to encourage anybody

00:59:53.790 --> 00:59:56.300
that has the least vanity to step forth.

00:59:56.300 --> 00:59:59.060
There's a total extinction of all tastes,

00:59:59.060 --> 01:00:01.810
our authors are vulgar, gross, illiberal.

01:00:01.810 --> 01:00:04.850
The theater swarms with
wretched translations

01:00:04.850 --> 01:00:07.040
and ballad operas and we have nothing new

01:00:07.040 --> 01:00:08.840
but improving abuse.

01:00:08.840 --> 01:00:11.780
I blushed at Paris when
the papers came over

01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:13.020
crowned with ribaldry

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:16.053
or with Garrick's insufferable
nonsense about Shakespeare.

01:00:17.042 --> 01:00:20.190
Garrick's prologues and
epilogues are as bad

01:00:20.190 --> 01:00:22.790
as his pindarics and pantomimes."

01:00:22.790 --> 01:00:24.510
He was on polite terms with Garrick

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:27.423
but he repeatedly
bad-mouthed him in private.

01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:31.780
So he didn't want his play to be mangled

01:00:31.780 --> 01:00:33.670
by professional performers.

01:00:33.670 --> 01:00:35.350
That's one of the worries I wouldn't deny

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:38.250
that the incest is affected too

01:00:38.250 --> 01:00:40.880
but he was really worried
about what would happen

01:00:40.880 --> 01:00:44.440
if his play was staged by professionals.

01:00:44.440 --> 01:00:46.590
And he wasn't the only one dissatisfied

01:00:46.590 --> 01:00:48.770
about the behavior of actors and audience

01:00:48.770 --> 01:00:50.560
in the patent theaters.

01:00:50.560 --> 01:00:52.490
"The Mysterious Mother"
I suggests can be seen

01:00:52.490 --> 01:00:54.620
as part of a larger
process which was occurring

01:00:54.620 --> 01:00:56.670
at the margins of professional theater

01:00:56.670 --> 01:00:59.630
challenging its economic
and cultural dominance.

01:00:59.630 --> 01:01:01.510
David Worrall, Jane Moody and others

01:01:01.510 --> 01:01:03.040
have documented the attack

01:01:03.040 --> 01:01:05.850
mounted against the
monopolies the patent theaters

01:01:05.850 --> 01:01:09.870
by the rambunctious minor
theaters in the late 18th century.

01:01:09.870 --> 01:01:11.880
To counter this threat
the patent theater staged

01:01:11.880 --> 01:01:13.890
more spectacular
productions and pantomimes

01:01:13.890 --> 01:01:16.930
to clawback the audience. And in response

01:01:16.930 --> 01:01:19.810
to the perceived increase in the vulgarity

01:01:19.810 --> 01:01:22.170
both of the productions
and of the audiences

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:24.520
at these ever bigger theaters

01:01:24.520 --> 01:01:26.390
at Covent Garden and Drury Lane,

01:01:26.390 --> 01:01:28.080
members of the social elite

01:01:28.080 --> 01:01:30.690
including a number of
Walpole's inner circle

01:01:30.690 --> 01:01:33.580
took to staging their
own private theatricals

01:01:33.580 --> 01:01:34.760
in which they could control

01:01:34.760 --> 01:01:37.260
both the repertoire and the audience,

01:01:37.260 --> 01:01:39.433
admitting only people they had vetted.

01:01:40.590 --> 01:01:43.130
Private theatricals became fashionable

01:01:43.130 --> 01:01:46.300
both because of their exclusivity

01:01:46.300 --> 01:01:48.670
and because of the performativity.

01:01:48.670 --> 01:01:52.373
The people who acted yes
they presumed you had a ball.

01:01:54.710 --> 01:01:59.300
Participating in culture is a major factor

01:01:59.300 --> 01:02:01.380
of the rise of private theatricals

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:04.960
but it's another factor is
this aristocratic desire

01:02:04.960 --> 01:02:08.050
to control what they perform
and who gets to see it.

01:02:09.520 --> 01:02:10.790
And these private theatricals

01:02:10.790 --> 01:02:13.410
became such a fixture
in the social calendar

01:02:13.410 --> 01:02:16.760
that they were announced in
the newspapers, in the listing.

01:02:16.760 --> 01:02:19.240
So all the major London
newspapers which would say

01:02:19.240 --> 01:02:21.560
what's on at Covent Garden,
what's on at Drury Lane,

01:02:21.560 --> 01:02:24.373
they'd then say what
Mrs. Hobart was doing.

01:02:25.247 --> 01:02:27.400
What was performing in
the Richmond House Theater

01:02:27.400 --> 01:02:30.200
they are listed on the front page

01:02:30.200 --> 01:02:33.140
and newspapers often carry examples

01:02:33.140 --> 01:02:35.260
with their prologues and epilogues,

01:02:35.260 --> 01:02:39.870
plot summaries, reviews, a list
of members of the audience.

01:02:39.870 --> 01:02:42.930
It's like those accounts of
big parties and premieres

01:02:42.930 --> 01:02:44.610
where all the guests are listed.

01:02:44.610 --> 01:02:46.820
So the names of the
guests are as important

01:02:46.820 --> 01:02:48.600
as the names of the performers

01:02:48.600 --> 01:02:52.020
And Walpole collected a
lot of this information

01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:55.630
in his massive collection
of plays of George III.

01:02:55.630 --> 01:02:57.740
There's one volume of
prologues and epilogues

01:02:57.740 --> 01:03:02.250
and its newspaper cuttings and
it indiscriminately includes

01:03:02.250 --> 01:03:05.310
both the patent theaters
and the private theaters.

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:09.360
He cuts and pastes the two
kinds of documents together.

01:03:09.360 --> 01:03:11.160
And a number of his letters give accounts

01:03:11.160 --> 01:03:13.583
of attending private theatricals.

01:03:14.680 --> 01:03:16.580
And a play that sort of pseudo play

01:03:16.580 --> 01:03:18.210
that's been mentioned a
couple of times today,

01:03:18.210 --> 01:03:22.750
"The Dear Witches" this sort
of satire based on Macbeth,

01:03:22.750 --> 01:03:25.690
it's a surprise theatrical.

01:03:25.690 --> 01:03:27.060
So it's not just a mock play

01:03:27.060 --> 01:03:29.630
but it's a mock private
theatrical performance

01:03:29.630 --> 01:03:31.460
of a version of Macbeth.

01:03:31.460 --> 01:03:34.460
So the sense of what a
private theatrical is

01:03:34.460 --> 01:03:38.240
informs his sense of
the theater generally.

01:03:38.240 --> 01:03:40.540
So the play text the
whole performance culture,

01:03:40.540 --> 01:03:41.693
the audience culture.

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:47.080
Now for Walpole these elite
amateurs not only embodied

01:03:47.080 --> 01:03:49.260
rather than performed their roles,

01:03:49.260 --> 01:03:52.270
they also embodied taste.

01:03:52.270 --> 01:03:54.670
The upper classes were
well schooled in music,

01:03:54.670 --> 01:03:57.490
dancing in the fine
arts and some were keen

01:03:57.490 --> 01:03:59.730
to stretch these accomplishments further.

01:03:59.730 --> 01:04:02.630
Mostly they adopted the
repertoire at the patent theaters

01:04:02.630 --> 01:04:06.110
producing a succession of
comedies and cut down Shakespeare.

01:04:06.110 --> 01:04:08.250
Some especially those in Walpole's circle

01:04:08.250 --> 01:04:09.350
wrote their own plays.

01:04:10.210 --> 01:04:11.920
Walpole's cousin Henry Seymour Conway

01:04:11.920 --> 01:04:14.240
performed his own adaptation
of "False Appearances"

01:04:14.240 --> 01:04:17.960
at Richmond House in 1788 and 1789.

01:04:17.960 --> 01:04:20.480
His daughter and Walpole's
heiress Anne Damer

01:04:20.480 --> 01:04:22.470
acted Mary Berry's "Fashionable Friends"

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:24.990
at Strawberry Hill in 1801.

01:04:24.990 --> 01:04:27.720
The most prolific amateur
playwright was Walpole's friend,

01:04:27.720 --> 01:04:29.960
Elizabeth Craven the
Margravine of Ansbach

01:04:29.960 --> 01:04:32.620
who wrote, produced and acted
in a succession of plays

01:04:32.620 --> 01:04:35.840
in her gothic Star Theatre at
Brandenburg House Hammersmith

01:04:35.840 --> 01:04:38.970
between about 1790 and 1805.

01:04:38.970 --> 01:04:40.020
Some of these scripts written

01:04:40.020 --> 01:04:41.900
by amateur aristocratic playwrights

01:04:41.900 --> 01:04:43.530
made it to the public theaters.

01:04:43.530 --> 01:04:45.310
Craven's comedy "The Miniature Picture"

01:04:45.310 --> 01:04:48.380
had a run of three nights
at Drury Lane in 1780

01:04:48.380 --> 01:04:50.370
and Conway's "False Appearances" enjoyed

01:04:50.370 --> 01:04:53.430
a run of six nights at
Drury Lane in April 1789

01:04:54.670 --> 01:04:57.220
and Walpole complained
that the play was adapted

01:04:57.220 --> 01:04:58.463
to get cheap laughs.

01:04:59.720 --> 01:05:02.510
So I think Walpole had no
opposition in principle

01:05:02.510 --> 01:05:04.610
to the social elites
writing for the public.

01:05:04.610 --> 01:05:07.230
He himself had an afterpiece 
"Nature Will Prevail,"

01:05:07.230 --> 01:05:09.900
performed regularly at the
Little Theater Hay Market

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:13.189
between 1778 and 1788.

01:05:13.189 --> 01:05:16.050
For him it was a question
of aesthetic quality,

01:05:16.050 --> 01:05:18.810
a quality measured by his taste.

01:05:18.810 --> 01:05:20.020
In a letter to Hannah Moore,

01:05:20.020 --> 01:05:23.330
he refers to a skirmish in print
about two noble playwrights

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:25.610
The Dukes of Newcastle and Buckingham.

01:05:25.610 --> 01:05:27.190
A gentleman essayist

01:05:28.202 --> 01:05:31.960
asked him why it was more ridiculous

01:05:31.960 --> 01:05:35.040
in the Duke of Newcastle
to write his two comedies

01:05:35.040 --> 01:05:37.677
than the Duke of Buckingham
to write "The Rehearsal."

01:05:37.677 --> 01:05:39.920
"Alas I know but one reason which is

01:05:39.920 --> 01:05:43.837
"that it is less ridiculous
to write one excellent comedy

01:05:43.837 --> 01:05:45.797
"than two very bad ones.

01:05:45.797 --> 01:05:47.947
"Peace be with such answerers."

01:05:48.810 --> 01:05:51.840
This dispute was prompted by
an account of these two Dukes

01:05:51.840 --> 01:05:55.200
and at the ancestors of the essayist William Belsham

01:05:55.200 --> 01:05:57.310
included by Walpole in his catalog

01:05:57.310 --> 01:05:59.490
"The Royal and Noble Authors of England,"

01:05:59.490 --> 01:06:01.440
an ever-expanding labor of love,

01:06:01.440 --> 01:06:04.150
first printed at Strawberry
Hill press in 1758

01:06:04.150 --> 01:06:07.943
and augmented and revised
in various reissues.

01:06:09.550 --> 01:06:11.840
In work such as his "Catalog
at the Royal and Noble

01:06:11.840 --> 01:06:13.430
authors of England" he builds a case

01:06:13.430 --> 01:06:15.910
with contribution of the
upper classes to culture

01:06:15.910 --> 01:06:19.060
as producers as well as
consumers of literature.

01:06:19.060 --> 01:06:20.930
He was not so blinded by his class.

01:06:20.930 --> 01:06:23.540
He could not see the faults of
his royal and noble authors.

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:25.580
He frequently gerdes himself up to produce

01:06:25.580 --> 01:06:28.140
his most withering wit at their expense.

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:29.444
For example after describing

01:06:29.444 --> 01:06:31.940
the writings of William
and Margaret Cavendish

01:06:31.940 --> 01:06:34.030
the Duke and Duchess of
Newcastle he declares,

01:06:34.030 --> 01:06:36.740
what a picture of foolish nobility

01:06:36.740 --> 01:06:38.540
was this stately poetic couple

01:06:38.540 --> 01:06:40.350
retired to their own little domain

01:06:40.350 --> 01:06:43.660
and intoxicating one another
with circumstantial flattery

01:06:43.660 --> 01:06:47.860
on what was consequence to
no mortal but themselves.

01:06:47.860 --> 01:06:49.570
Well people in stained glass houses

01:06:49.570 --> 01:06:51.993
should not throw the
first stone Mr. Walpole.

01:06:53.240 --> 01:06:56.270
Yet he values this category
of writers for two reasons.

01:06:56.270 --> 01:06:58.680
The first is that there
are not career writers.

01:06:58.680 --> 01:07:01.160
Their authorship was
secondary to or sometimes

01:07:01.160 --> 01:07:03.800
the product of their service to the state.

01:07:03.800 --> 01:07:06.070
The second is that their
independence from the market

01:07:06.070 --> 01:07:07.780
in his view made them better able

01:07:07.780 --> 01:07:10.253
to establish standards
of tastes and excellence.

01:07:11.180 --> 01:07:12.920
Walpole resisted public performance

01:07:12.920 --> 01:07:15.790
because he deplored the
current state of the theater.

01:07:15.790 --> 01:07:18.110
Writing to Robert Jephson and 1777

01:07:18.110 --> 01:07:20.740
about this gentleman
authors new play Battaglia

01:07:20.740 --> 01:07:22.927
Walpole declared, "If I were the author,

01:07:22.927 --> 01:07:25.587
"I should be very sorry to
have my tragedy murdered

01:07:25.587 --> 01:07:27.667
"perhaps miscarry and to give your work

01:07:27.667 --> 01:07:30.737
"strong, worthy performers is
like sacrificing a daughter

01:07:30.737 --> 01:07:32.817
"to her husband of bad character."

01:07:34.030 --> 01:07:35.520
Actors were not the only problem.

01:07:35.520 --> 01:07:37.940
Walpole also directed
blame at theater managers

01:07:37.940 --> 01:07:40.470
who because of their
enslavement to convention

01:07:40.470 --> 01:07:42.530
and dependence on the
box office were unwilling

01:07:42.530 --> 01:07:45.520
to foster truly original
writing on the stage.

01:07:45.520 --> 01:07:48.090
Among the reasons he
gave to Montagu in 1768

01:07:48.090 --> 01:07:50.420
for not putting his Mysterious
Mother on the stage,

01:07:50.420 --> 01:07:54.727
Walpole fulminated, "Nor am
I disposed to expose myself

01:07:54.727 --> 01:07:57.947
"to that jackanapes Garrick
who lets nothing appear

01:07:57.947 --> 01:07:59.777
"but his own wretched stuff

01:07:59.777 --> 01:08:02.387
"or that of creatures still duller,

01:08:02.387 --> 01:08:05.977
"who suffer him to alter
their pieces as he pleases."

01:08:07.389 --> 01:08:10.320
He encouraged Jephson to
produce his "Count of Narbonne"

01:08:10.320 --> 01:08:11.930
which we've heard of already,

01:08:11.930 --> 01:08:14.727
not at Covent Garden
where it was staged in 1781

01:08:14.727 --> 01:08:16.480
but in his hometown of Dublin

01:08:17.560 --> 01:08:19.137
where he could oversee rehearsals.

01:08:19.137 --> 01:08:22.150
Managers will take
liberties and often curtail

01:08:22.150 --> 01:08:24.849
necessary speeches so
as to produce nonsense.

01:08:24.849 --> 01:08:27.074
Take warning David.

01:08:27.074 --> 01:08:30.270
Methinks it is unkind to send a child

01:08:30.270 --> 01:08:32.530
of which you have so
much reason to be proud

01:08:32.530 --> 01:08:34.230
to a foundling hospital.

01:08:34.230 --> 01:08:37.780
So lots and lots of
metaphors of work has baby,

01:08:37.780 --> 01:08:39.173
of murder and so on.

01:08:40.390 --> 01:08:43.750
So for Walpole the beautiful
negligence of a gentleman

01:08:43.750 --> 01:08:45.620
represented a freedom from rules

01:08:45.620 --> 01:08:47.610
and the liberty to experiment.

01:08:47.610 --> 01:08:49.620
Repeatedly he objected the way

01:08:49.620 --> 01:08:51.680
the French neoclassical unities of time,

01:08:51.680 --> 01:08:54.030
place and action were
imposed on British drama.

01:08:56.330 --> 01:08:57.163
I'll skip this quotation.

01:08:57.163 --> 01:08:59.410
It's a lovely quotation about cats.

01:08:59.410 --> 01:09:01.400
In his pocketbook but I
don't need some move on.

01:09:01.400 --> 01:09:03.610
I want to get a cat into
every lecture I give.

01:09:03.610 --> 01:09:06.280
Usually show a photograph
of my own cat at some point.

01:09:06.280 --> 01:09:08.033
Anyway moving swiftly on.

01:09:09.372 --> 01:09:13.470
So we've heard about the
PostScript to the 1781,

01:09:13.470 --> 01:09:15.910
printing "The Mysterious Mother."

01:09:15.910 --> 01:09:17.770
I'll just pick out a few
phrases, he talked about

01:09:17.770 --> 01:09:19.790
people being enslaved to rules,

01:09:19.790 --> 01:09:21.980
he prefers an ample attitude of thought

01:09:21.980 --> 01:09:23.560
and pursuance of which is introduce

01:09:23.560 --> 01:09:25.420
some novelty on our stage.

01:09:25.420 --> 01:09:27.583
He boasts that the cast of the whole play

01:09:27.583 --> 01:09:32.583
unlike any other that I'm acquainted with.

01:09:33.640 --> 01:09:37.360
In particular the character
the countess is certainly new.

01:09:37.360 --> 01:09:40.110
Novelty, originality, freedom.

01:09:40.110 --> 01:09:41.730
These are his watch words.

01:09:41.730 --> 01:09:45.010
Mostly Walpole asserts
these freedoms for himself.

01:09:45.010 --> 01:09:48.210
But I think that he hints that
by exercising such freedoms,

01:09:48.210 --> 01:09:49.590
gentlemen might raise standards

01:09:49.590 --> 01:09:51.493
of tastes and reform in the theater.

01:09:52.930 --> 01:09:57.930
Now I just want to end
by turning to Cynthia

01:09:58.130 --> 01:10:01.390
that some of my impetus
for this work on Walpole

01:10:01.390 --> 01:10:04.010
has come from her wonderful article

01:10:04.010 --> 01:10:06.900
on Walpole and Female Genius

01:10:06.900 --> 01:10:11.900
where she talks about how
Walpole's championing of lady Di

01:10:12.850 --> 01:10:17.030
who did those incredible
suit water drawings.

01:10:17.030 --> 01:10:21.790
He was interested in a
number of women geniuses

01:10:21.790 --> 01:10:24.000
many of whom were on that sort of fringes

01:10:24.000 --> 01:10:26.630
a respectability or
downright unrespectable

01:10:27.700 --> 01:10:29.660
and he valued their lack of training,

01:10:29.660 --> 01:10:31.183
their leisure deeds and their independence

01:10:31.183 --> 01:10:35.990
from the marketplace and
that's a lot of his emphasis

01:10:35.990 --> 01:10:40.190
in championing them was to
oppose academic standards.

01:10:40.190 --> 01:10:42.470
The standards of the Royal Academy.

01:10:42.470 --> 01:10:46.670
And I would like to suggest
that his attitude to the theater

01:10:46.670 --> 01:10:48.180
was very similar.

01:10:48.180 --> 01:10:52.068
He's insisting on developing
a kind of an amateur culture

01:10:52.068 --> 01:10:57.068
in the theater as well as an
amateur culture in the arts.

01:10:57.310 --> 01:11:01.460
Although we don't
expect necessarily to find

01:11:01.460 --> 01:11:05.160
the avant-garde in the amateur sphere,

01:11:05.160 --> 01:11:09.130
I do think that the private theatricals

01:11:09.130 --> 01:11:13.830
might allow us to rethink the significance

01:11:13.830 --> 01:11:16.677
of the emergence of the
professional theater

01:11:16.677 --> 01:11:20.510
and the kind of complexity
that it entails.

01:11:20.510 --> 01:11:22.160
Thank you very much.

01:11:22.160 --> 01:11:24.410
(applause)

01:11:32.500 --> 01:11:36.330
- Thank you and one quick
PSA before we go on.

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:37.960
If you saw me pulling
my cell phone out here,

01:11:37.960 --> 01:11:39.800
I promise it wasn't texting my friends.

01:11:39.800 --> 01:11:43.620
If you are interested in
promoting any of the pictures,

01:11:43.620 --> 01:11:46.640
ideas or images that you've been a part of

01:11:46.640 --> 01:11:49.510
these last two days, you can tweet it

01:11:49.510 --> 01:11:52.470
and there's a hashtag Walpolooza

01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:56.860
and so if you hashtag it
then maybe you can see

01:11:56.860 --> 01:12:00.133
what others saw and
captured and reflected on.

01:12:01.170 --> 01:12:02.360
All right, wonderful.

01:12:02.360 --> 01:12:07.360
Well I get to introduce my new colleague

01:12:08.370 --> 01:12:11.880
and fellow script
conspirator, David Worrall

01:12:11.880 --> 01:12:15.880
who gave us that fantastic
cut of "The Mysterious Mother"

01:12:15.880 --> 01:12:18.580
and chiseled a playable shorter piece

01:12:18.580 --> 01:12:21.150
out of Walpole's longer work.

01:12:21.150 --> 01:12:23.720
David is emeritus professor of English

01:12:23.720 --> 01:12:26.070
at Nottingham Trent University England.

01:12:26.070 --> 01:12:29.210
He's published widely on
long 18th century theater

01:12:29.210 --> 01:12:31.540
including theatric revolution

01:12:31.540 --> 01:12:33.912
and celebrity performance and reception.

01:12:33.912 --> 01:12:37.060
He's held grants, fellowships
and has more plaudits

01:12:37.060 --> 01:12:40.299
than we have time left today

01:12:40.299 --> 01:12:44.170
but these include the Leverhulme Trust,

01:12:44.170 --> 01:12:46.070
the Arts and Humanities Research Council

01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:48.210
and The Lewis Walpole Library Fellowship.

01:12:49.270 --> 01:12:53.040
And today he is going to
speak to us about the play

01:12:53.040 --> 01:12:56.780
under the title, "'I beg you would
keep it under lock and key':

01:12:56.780 --> 01:12:59.380
the mystery of the 1821

01:12:59.380 --> 01:13:01.523
'Mysterious Mother' performances."

01:13:07.250 --> 01:13:09.882
- So again if I can say

01:13:09.882 --> 01:13:13.063
it's been an excellent
opportunity to come here

01:13:13.063 --> 01:13:18.063
that invitation from Cindy
and the Walpole library

01:13:21.720 --> 01:13:26.580
and of course having been involved in it

01:13:26.580 --> 01:13:30.010
right from the start with Misty.

01:13:30.010 --> 01:13:32.110
So it seems like along time ago

01:13:32.110 --> 01:13:35.950
I was presented the
opportunity to do these things

01:13:35.950 --> 01:13:38.690
and so these things and
so probably talking about

01:13:38.690 --> 01:13:41.620
in fact is some of the things

01:13:41.620 --> 01:13:44.270
which have been discussed earlier.

01:13:44.270 --> 01:13:47.980
So I hope that as I'm the last speaker,

01:13:47.980 --> 01:13:50.700
this would be light but not lightweight.

01:13:50.700 --> 01:13:52.760
That's my kind of aim really.

01:13:54.150 --> 01:13:58.510
But to go back to look at
it the other way around.

01:14:01.770 --> 01:14:05.360
So really what I'm talking
about is not the way in which

01:14:05.360 --> 01:14:08.800
"The Mysterious Mother"
was controlled by Walpole

01:14:08.800 --> 01:14:10.790
but really how it escapes.

01:14:10.790 --> 01:14:14.650
So its escape from control
really is the story

01:14:14.650 --> 01:14:18.600
that I have to tell in kind of two halves.

01:14:18.600 --> 01:14:23.380
So this is really I
guess so September 1769

01:14:23.380 --> 01:14:28.380
is the first read-through
of "The Mysterious Mother"

01:14:29.130 --> 01:14:33.100
and I'll show you on the next slide

01:14:33.100 --> 01:14:35.870
but these are the personnel involved

01:14:35.870 --> 01:14:37.990
in George Montagu's house.

01:14:37.990 --> 01:14:40.950
"I've had such a houseful
schoolboys for a week

01:14:40.950 --> 01:14:43.420
that they're noise still
tingles in my ears.

01:14:43.420 --> 01:14:47.080
Thank God their holidays
are over and they are gone."

01:14:47.080 --> 01:14:49.770
So George Osborne, Colonel Burgoyne

01:14:49.770 --> 01:14:53.110
which is not the one who'd
surrendered at Saratoga

01:14:53.110 --> 01:14:55.110
because I thought there'd
be an American link

01:14:55.110 --> 01:14:56.370
but there's not.

01:14:56.370 --> 01:15:00.770
Captain Bosco and Young
Osborn, a parson of theirs

01:15:00.770 --> 01:15:02.130
and one of their squires

01:15:02.130 --> 01:15:05.570
came all upon me on a
sudden and live riotously

01:15:05.570 --> 01:15:09.090
from morning to night.
So that's the context

01:15:09.090 --> 01:15:13.950
of how they then do this read through.

01:15:13.950 --> 01:15:17.420
"The boys found your tragedy
"The Mysterious Mother"

01:15:17.420 --> 01:15:20.850
and the boys as they
are they had no patience

01:15:20.850 --> 01:15:24.160
till they finished it and
we read the acts amongst us

01:15:24.160 --> 01:15:28.160
for my lungs could not
Pritchard it through.

01:15:28.160 --> 01:15:31.100
They left off quinze gambling and claret

01:15:31.100 --> 01:15:34.560
and were the Florian's of your play,

01:15:34.560 --> 01:15:36.920
the churchmen indeed look grave,

01:15:36.920 --> 01:15:40.930
but they threw him his Ten
Commandments and a bottle of port

01:15:40.930 --> 01:15:43.240
and amused themselves without.

01:15:43.240 --> 01:15:45.880
They would have got it by heart however,

01:15:45.880 --> 01:15:48.200
a few similes they have retained

01:15:48.200 --> 01:15:49.350
and a cap with one another

01:15:49.350 --> 01:15:52.380
some of the lines that struck the most."

01:15:52.380 --> 01:15:55.020
So what we've got there is so first of all

01:15:55.020 --> 01:15:58.640
they kind of captured a character
which is the Florian one

01:15:58.640 --> 01:16:01.030
so that's the first
reception history if you like

01:16:01.030 --> 01:16:04.020
of Florian as a character. And of course

01:16:04.020 --> 01:16:06.530
what he's saying is that
they've learned by heart

01:16:06.530 --> 01:16:10.470
some of the speeches and of course

01:16:10.470 --> 01:16:12.550
they seem to have read through it.

01:16:12.550 --> 01:16:16.320
So it's in that kind of riotous context.

01:16:16.320 --> 01:16:21.320
So already within a year
"The Mysterious Mother"

01:16:21.320 --> 01:16:23.070
is kind of out there

01:16:23.070 --> 01:16:26.681
amongst that very mixed
group of individuals

01:16:26.681 --> 01:16:31.681
who seem not least completely
fully at ease with it.

01:16:31.690 --> 01:16:33.820
They're having a very good time.

01:16:33.820 --> 01:16:38.800
So there's none of these kind
of darker aspects about incest

01:16:38.800 --> 01:16:40.460
or gothic or any of the things

01:16:40.460 --> 01:16:44.040
which we may associate with it.

01:16:44.040 --> 01:16:46.120
They associate the play

01:16:46.120 --> 01:16:48.820
and they're the first people to read it

01:16:48.820 --> 01:16:53.820
and to act or read it through
a staged reading perhaps.

01:16:54.222 --> 01:16:59.222
They begin the reception
characteristics of that drama

01:17:01.770 --> 01:17:05.700
and so we tend to have forgotten this.

01:17:05.700 --> 01:17:10.010
It seems to me that the private sit

01:17:10.010 --> 01:17:13.280
in that private theatrical mode in fact

01:17:13.280 --> 01:17:17.100
the play has escaped and
it's become something

01:17:17.100 --> 01:17:20.800
which of course Horace has no control over

01:17:20.800 --> 01:17:25.800
and so there of course we get
him after the horse has bolted

01:17:25.990 --> 01:17:29.070
as you may think is
the thing. And of course

01:17:29.070 --> 01:17:32.450
that's my point really is
that these famous lines

01:17:32.450 --> 01:17:35.280
about keeping it under lock and key.

01:17:35.280 --> 01:17:36.620
One needs to look back

01:17:36.620 --> 01:17:41.450
at what they have actually
done with the text

01:17:41.450 --> 01:17:44.110
and in fact you know although he says

01:17:44.110 --> 01:17:47.050
to keep it under lock and key

01:17:47.050 --> 01:17:50.290
in fact it has already escaped.

01:17:50.290 --> 01:17:54.220
There wasn't a lock in the
first place if you'd like

01:17:54.220 --> 01:17:58.390
and so that really gives
the prompt for this way

01:17:58.390 --> 01:18:02.330
in which that first
audience and players in it

01:18:02.330 --> 01:18:05.340
are completely ease with this play is that

01:18:05.340 --> 01:18:09.860
this is how this culture
is receiving this play

01:18:11.050 --> 01:18:14.770
as something that they can
enjoy as an entertainment

01:18:14.770 --> 01:18:16.690
and something which they can learn

01:18:16.690 --> 01:18:19.710
and that they are comfortable with.

01:18:19.710 --> 01:18:21.780
So unless it's some other version

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:24.840
of "The Mysterious Mother" that they've got

01:18:25.690 --> 01:18:30.233
but I don't think there's
any evidence for that.

01:18:31.550 --> 01:18:34.370
That's what we know about that.

01:18:34.370 --> 01:18:39.370
And so the thing that I
came across some years ago

01:18:40.600 --> 01:18:44.160
is the venue of the first performance

01:18:44.160 --> 01:18:46.150
which is at the Surrey Theatre, 

01:18:46.150 --> 01:18:49.950
one of the minor or illegitimate theaters

01:18:49.950 --> 01:18:53.960
on the south side of the Thames.
And that's really important

01:18:53.960 --> 01:18:57.010
it's on the south side of the
Thames because what it meant

01:18:57.010 --> 01:19:00.180
is that they didn't have
to send a licensing copy

01:19:00.180 --> 01:19:03.560
to the Lord Chamberlain because
the Lord Chamberlain's remit

01:19:03.560 --> 01:19:08.040
only ran in Westminster on
the North side of the Thames.

01:19:08.040 --> 01:19:11.910
So we don't have a text, manuscript text

01:19:11.910 --> 01:19:16.601
for the performance that
they did for that reason

01:19:16.601 --> 01:19:20.350
because it escapes through that way.

01:19:20.350 --> 01:19:23.350
So the manager of it is Thomas Dibdin.

01:19:23.350 --> 01:19:25.960
So he's an important
character because he's the one

01:19:25.960 --> 01:19:29.220
who's probably done the first cut of it

01:19:29.220 --> 01:19:31.920
as I'll begin to show.

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:36.000
So that's the theater previously
known as the Royal Circus

01:19:36.000 --> 01:19:38.950
and it's part of that
expansion in the minor

01:19:38.950 --> 01:19:42.567
or the illegitimate theaters
that begins around 1818

01:19:44.448 --> 01:19:48.400
with the opening of
Waterloo Bridge in 1818

01:19:48.400 --> 01:19:51.690
because from then when
Waterloo Bridge opens up.

01:19:51.690 --> 01:19:54.820
You can walk from Covent
Garden and Drury Lane

01:19:54.820 --> 01:19:56.700
across to the other side of the river

01:19:56.700 --> 01:20:00.470
As you may know the present
Old Vic is the Old Coburg

01:20:00.470 --> 01:20:02.940
is the Royal Coburg theater.

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:06.320
That's the same site
and these two the Surrey

01:20:06.320 --> 01:20:08.980
and the present-day Old Vic, we'll call it

01:20:08.980 --> 01:20:13.130
the Royal Coburg Theatre, were
in competition with each other.

01:20:13.130 --> 01:20:16.030
So not only do you have this
opening up a new theaters

01:20:16.030 --> 01:20:18.595
on the South side of the Thames

01:20:18.595 --> 01:20:21.150
but also elements of competition.

01:20:21.150 --> 01:20:25.410
So it's a very vigorous
community of theaters,

01:20:25.410 --> 01:20:28.071
not a community. They
kind of hated each other.

01:20:28.071 --> 01:20:31.810
But trying to find new material

01:20:31.810 --> 01:20:34.460
and that this is new
material that they found.

01:20:34.460 --> 01:20:38.150
Now when I published the essay,

01:20:38.150 --> 01:20:41.350
I didn't have an image
included in the essay

01:20:42.780 --> 01:20:45.280
of the post of the playbill

01:20:46.890 --> 01:20:51.890
and I only refound it recently
in the bowels of my computer.

01:20:52.220 --> 01:20:55.940
So that's I think the next exhibit.

01:20:55.940 --> 01:21:00.940
So it's a photograph of microfilm.

01:21:01.360 --> 01:21:03.260
So because there were 10 performances,

01:21:03.260 --> 01:21:04.770
there were 10 of these.

01:21:04.770 --> 01:21:07.870
Of course I'm only showing
you the first one there

01:21:07.870 --> 01:21:11.480
and I'm just gonna blow that up a bit.

01:21:11.480 --> 01:21:13.000
And so you can see that

01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:17.330
there's no kind of ambiguity about that.

01:21:17.330 --> 01:21:21.820
The serious melodramatic
romance in three acts

01:21:21.820 --> 01:21:26.820
founded on a tragic play written
by the late Horace Walpole

01:21:26.820 --> 01:21:31.820
Earl of Orford and now
presented for the first time

01:21:32.140 --> 01:21:34.820
as it says never acted
before under the title

01:21:34.820 --> 01:21:38.140
of Narbonne Castle or
the Mysterious Mother.

01:21:38.140 --> 01:21:41.015
There is no ambiguity
about what that play is

01:21:41.015 --> 01:21:45.743
and that is exactly what the play is.

01:21:46.800 --> 01:21:49.550
You see that as it goes on to say

01:21:49.550 --> 01:21:53.780
a noble author, maybe he
refers to the Byron preface

01:21:53.780 --> 01:21:55.403
to Marino Faliero.

01:22:01.330 --> 01:22:03.450
So that's just kind of cue if you like

01:22:03.450 --> 01:22:06.940
and he goes on to say "there
are however in the fable

01:22:06.940 --> 01:22:10.473
circumstances of a dreadfully terrific,

01:22:11.630 --> 01:22:14.610
of so dreadfully terrific in nature

01:22:14.610 --> 01:22:19.610
that most [indistinct] material
alteration in its construction

01:22:20.360 --> 01:22:23.660
has been thought necessary.

01:22:23.660 --> 01:22:26.730
Now first of all because it's a theater

01:22:26.730 --> 01:22:29.060
on the South side of the Thames,

01:22:29.060 --> 01:22:31.120
they have to put music in.

01:22:31.120 --> 01:22:32.420
Now that's not a choice.

01:22:32.420 --> 01:22:34.980
It's not an elective choice.

01:22:34.980 --> 01:22:38.530
That is because the theaters
on the South side of the Thames

01:22:40.046 --> 01:22:43.690
and the theaters outside of
Covent Garden and Drury Lane

01:22:43.690 --> 01:22:48.490
if they have to do musicalized versions.

01:22:48.490 --> 01:22:52.362
So the patentees at Covent
Garden and Drury Lane

01:22:52.362 --> 01:22:56.740
who have the monopoly on
speech protect their monopoly

01:22:56.740 --> 01:23:00.720
by oppressing the minor
theaters and certainly

01:23:00.720 --> 01:23:05.720
in Dibdin's case he had spies
around from the patentees

01:23:08.990 --> 01:23:10.200
to see what he was doing.

01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:13.410
They were trying to close
him down essentially

01:23:13.410 --> 01:23:17.560
and they were trying to
close down the others.

01:23:17.560 --> 01:23:21.903
So because but the patentees thought that

01:23:21.903 --> 01:23:24.870
they were playing spoken drama,

01:23:24.870 --> 01:23:29.170
so this is a musicalized
version of the play.

01:23:29.170 --> 01:23:31.632
Contracted it to three acts,

01:23:31.632 --> 01:23:34.520
what I want to emphasize is that

01:23:34.520 --> 01:23:38.060
this is not something
that they elected to do.

01:23:38.060 --> 01:23:42.310
This was something because of
the regulatory circumstances

01:23:42.310 --> 01:23:45.660
that they had to do and so probably

01:23:45.660 --> 01:23:48.460
the reason why it contracts the three acts

01:23:48.460 --> 01:23:50.930
is because once you've added the music

01:23:50.930 --> 01:23:54.170
which they needed to keep their license

01:23:55.230 --> 01:23:59.330
of course the play, the whole
play takes a lot more time

01:23:59.330 --> 01:24:02.220
and of course he would have seen as I saw

01:24:02.220 --> 01:24:05.030
that it's eminently cuttable anyway

01:24:05.030 --> 01:24:10.030
and so that is what he was doing there

01:24:11.040 --> 01:24:15.510
and the other thing that I
found out only the other day

01:24:15.510 --> 01:24:18.881
since I've been here
where I hadn't realized

01:24:18.881 --> 01:24:21.373
that the time have brought
out the Journal article.

01:24:24.691 --> 01:24:25.524
In 1819 Dibdin had tried
to lease Drury Lane

01:24:31.210 --> 01:24:36.210
and so Farmington the
Walpole library in 2017

01:24:36.310 --> 01:24:40.000
bought some new manuscripts which exposed

01:24:40.000 --> 01:24:44.320
and which go through his
attempts to take over

01:24:44.320 --> 01:24:47.030
the lease of Drury Lane which he failed.

01:24:47.030 --> 01:24:48.760
What would have happened to Dibdin

01:24:48.760 --> 01:24:52.960
is that he'd raised from a linen merchant

01:24:52.960 --> 01:24:57.960
in the Strand [indistinct]
warehouse 10,000 pounds

01:24:58.470 --> 01:25:00.680
to pay for the lease plus Drury Lane

01:25:00.680 --> 01:25:05.370
wanted him to pay 3000 pounds
to refurbish the theater.

01:25:05.370 --> 01:25:07.493
So although the new theater,

01:25:07.493 --> 01:25:12.493
the newly refurbished Drury
Lane had opened in 1812, 1813.

01:25:17.336 --> 01:25:20.730
By the time that he comes
to take over the lease

01:25:20.730 --> 01:25:25.150
or try to take over the lease,
it had fallen into disrepair

01:25:25.150 --> 01:25:29.990
despite Edmund Kean and all of that.

01:25:29.990 --> 01:25:33.670
Anyway to cut a long story
short it came to sealed bids

01:25:33.670 --> 01:25:36.480
but he was not there
when the bids were opened

01:25:36.480 --> 01:25:40.010
and so according to Dibdin,
he felt that he'd been cheated

01:25:40.010 --> 01:25:43.170
that somebody at the last stage
had opened up a sealed bid

01:25:43.170 --> 01:25:45.660
and then put in a 100 pounds more

01:25:45.660 --> 01:25:49.390
and so he had every reason

01:25:49.390 --> 01:25:53.549
to be antagonistic against Drury Lane.

01:25:53.549 --> 01:25:58.549
The reason I think he
chooses to do it is that

01:25:58.605 --> 01:26:02.620
it's a spoiling attempt on Marino Falieri

01:26:03.950 --> 01:26:06.330
which Drury Lane is bringing out.

01:26:06.330 --> 01:26:09.620
The circumstances of that play is that

01:26:12.895 --> 01:26:16.250
the publisher tries to bring an injunction

01:26:16.250 --> 01:26:21.250
against Drury Lane to
stop them staging the play

01:26:21.840 --> 01:26:25.540
Byron of course as you know
isn't in the country anyway.

01:26:25.540 --> 01:26:28.540
The license from the Lord Chamberlain

01:26:28.540 --> 01:26:32.083
comes back to Drury Lane
but the servants don't tell

01:26:32.083 --> 01:26:36.460
the manager that the
license is in the playhouse

01:26:36.460 --> 01:26:41.060
and so that afternoon they
couldn't stage it anyway

01:26:41.060 --> 01:26:44.311
so even though the
injunction hasn't been heard,

01:26:44.311 --> 01:26:48.080
Drury Lane in other words are themselves

01:26:48.080 --> 01:26:51.740
playing a dangerous game. And
so what you've got really

01:26:51.740 --> 01:26:55.410
is a way in which all these
theaters are competing

01:26:55.410 --> 01:26:59.030
in underhand ways against each other.

01:26:59.030 --> 01:27:01.452
And so in a way "The Mysterious Mother"

01:27:01.452 --> 01:27:06.452
is part of that attempt by
Dibdin to get his own back

01:27:07.560 --> 01:27:12.560
on Drury Lane where he had
these really awful experiences.

01:27:14.140 --> 01:27:16.700
But this is my main point really.

01:27:16.700 --> 01:27:18.733
It is a three-act version.

01:27:19.910 --> 01:27:21.940
We can think of good reasons

01:27:21.940 --> 01:27:24.780
why it should be a three-act version.

01:27:24.780 --> 01:27:29.360
It's also musicalized because
of the regulatory environment.

01:27:29.360 --> 01:27:32.180
It's not something that
he would have chosen

01:27:32.180 --> 01:27:37.180
to do himself perhaps and so

01:27:37.472 --> 01:27:41.590
that's how that came about there.

01:27:41.590 --> 01:27:44.920
And so what I've tried to do meanwhile

01:27:44.920 --> 01:27:48.690
and this is really the end piece really

01:27:48.690 --> 01:27:53.690
is that this is the first
actress to play the countess.

01:27:54.470 --> 01:27:58.510
So this actress is the one
who establishes the role

01:27:58.510 --> 01:28:01.070
in theater history.

01:28:01.070 --> 01:28:05.860
Miss Margaret Taylor and
that's an image of her

01:28:06.879 --> 01:28:08.870
and as you see there,
that's Miss Margaret Taylor

01:28:08.870 --> 01:28:13.660
of the Surrey theater and there she is.

01:28:13.660 --> 01:28:18.660
She's referred to there
Jacqueline Countess of Narbonne.

01:28:20.376 --> 01:28:24.330
"The Mysterious Mother"
is played by Miss Taylor

01:28:24.330 --> 01:28:27.290
and there we have that actress

01:28:27.290 --> 01:28:32.290
located with this Surrey theater there.

01:28:32.440 --> 01:28:35.860
And so I was trying to do some
calculations the other day

01:28:35.860 --> 01:28:39.060
and if you accept my interpretation

01:28:39.060 --> 01:28:44.060
that first 1769 read-through
would also have had

01:28:45.580 --> 01:28:47.950
somebody playing the countess.

01:28:47.950 --> 01:28:52.950
So all those male guys who
were in that house that night

01:28:53.200 --> 01:28:56.350
again they're very
comfortable with playing

01:28:56.350 --> 01:28:58.780
a big female role.

01:28:58.780 --> 01:29:02.100
This makes by my calculation
because you've got them

01:29:02.100 --> 01:29:05.610
so we capped the
schoolboys as we call them.

01:29:05.610 --> 01:29:07.100
So that's the first countess.

01:29:07.100 --> 01:29:10.350
We don't know who that one would have been

01:29:10.350 --> 01:29:14.770
and then we've got that
one, that's number two

01:29:14.770 --> 01:29:19.400
then there's Glasgow production in 2001

01:29:19.400 --> 01:29:24.350
and so Georgina here
is the fourth countess.

01:29:24.350 --> 01:29:26.720
And that's the end of my talk.

01:29:26.720 --> 01:29:27.768
Thanks very much.

01:29:27.768 --> 01:29:30.018
(applause)

01:29:33.560 --> 01:29:35.170
- Thanks so much everyone.

01:29:35.170 --> 01:29:38.638
I would like to invite
our speakers up here.

01:29:38.638 --> 01:29:40.805
(mumbles)

01:29:46.490 --> 01:29:49.210
Is there anyone who would like

01:29:49.210 --> 01:29:50.653
to lead off the questioning?

01:29:53.440 --> 01:29:55.950
We have once again the handheld mics

01:29:55.950 --> 01:29:57.370
and we do ask that you wait

01:29:57.370 --> 01:29:59.070
until the microphone gets to you

01:29:59.070 --> 01:30:01.570
because we're recording
this for posterity.

01:30:01.570 --> 01:30:03.444
The countess has a question.

01:30:03.444 --> 01:30:05.570
(laughs)

01:30:05.570 --> 01:30:06.403
- [Georgina] Thank you.

01:30:06.403 --> 01:30:10.720
It looks to me as though
that first production

01:30:10.720 --> 01:30:12.720
must have been a huge amount of fun

01:30:12.720 --> 01:30:15.953
with people sharing the part of Pritchard.

01:30:17.130 --> 01:30:19.470
Just maybe passing it around them

01:30:19.470 --> 01:30:23.410
and probably competing to
see who could do her best

01:30:23.410 --> 01:30:24.820
perhaps moving it as well.

01:30:24.820 --> 01:30:27.540
And the other thing I wanted to ask you,

01:30:27.540 --> 01:30:28.690
perhaps I should have asked at home

01:30:28.690 --> 01:30:33.690
but we didn't get time is the
adaptations made to the script

01:30:36.860 --> 01:30:40.890
the way that they kind of
parts have been blinged up

01:30:40.890 --> 01:30:45.692
so that the the plot, the gothic
plot is kind of foreground.

01:30:45.692 --> 01:30:49.283
You've got the confesser
and this kind of stuff.

01:30:52.170 --> 01:30:54.078
- Yes, the spectral monk.

01:30:54.078 --> 01:30:56.218
(mumbles)

01:30:56.218 --> 01:30:57.051
- [Female] Have you got any sense

01:30:57.051 --> 01:30:59.397
or is there any script at all,

01:30:59.397 --> 01:31:01.120
anything that suggests

01:31:01.120 --> 01:31:04.180
what they would might have been reviews?

01:31:04.180 --> 01:31:08.670
- There are a few reviews
that I've been able to find.

01:31:08.670 --> 01:31:10.800
Again you know there are no complaints.

01:31:10.800 --> 01:31:13.990
Nobody complaints--lines
up outside the theater

01:31:13.990 --> 01:31:18.990
and so it was reviewed but
there are not many reviews

01:31:19.170 --> 01:31:23.180
and generally you know
because it plays for 10 nights

01:31:24.250 --> 01:31:27.523
it's just a perfectly
reasonably good success story

01:31:30.210 --> 01:31:32.818
for that particular playhouse.

01:31:32.818 --> 01:31:37.333
So it's just kind of average.

01:31:38.460 --> 01:31:42.570
It's nothing special in their run

01:31:43.574 --> 01:31:46.220
of productions at that time.

01:31:46.220 --> 01:31:51.170
I mean Dipdin was doing
more experimental drama.

01:31:51.170 --> 01:31:56.170
I think the one that I worked on before

01:31:57.530 --> 01:31:59.980
is called something like "The Fall of Troy"

01:31:59.980 --> 01:32:03.550
where he manages to refer to to the Sun

01:32:03.550 --> 01:32:06.470
and the Norwich Union insurance companies

01:32:06.470 --> 01:32:10.140
because they're obviously
sponsoring him to do that

01:32:10.140 --> 01:32:12.890
so it's that kind of theater if you like

01:32:12.890 --> 01:32:16.980
so it's a very kind of normal
but it's not Covent Garden

01:32:16.980 --> 01:32:19.473
or Drury Lane type of thing.

01:32:20.330 --> 01:32:23.390
- I would just say it's so tantalizing

01:32:23.390 --> 01:32:26.650
you get these little traces
that seem to speak so big

01:32:26.650 --> 01:32:29.240
and there's a spectral
monk in that production

01:32:29.240 --> 01:32:31.730
it seems like pretty much
I said about the play

01:32:31.730 --> 01:32:36.600
in 1768 is not applying
and it would presumably be

01:32:36.600 --> 01:32:40.380
sort of a part and parcel
with at that point,

01:32:40.380 --> 01:32:43.540
a robust sort of Gothic drama tradition

01:32:43.540 --> 01:32:45.470
and the question that I
wouldn't be able answer

01:32:45.470 --> 01:32:47.490
as to what extent is the stage craft

01:32:47.490 --> 01:32:49.770
and you know special effects that mark

01:32:49.770 --> 01:32:51.240
the you know the patent theaters.

01:32:51.240 --> 01:32:53.743
To what extent was that
replicable at Surrey.

01:32:57.090 --> 01:32:58.620
- The playbill does also suggest

01:32:58.620 --> 01:33:01.581
that the plot has been censored.

01:33:01.581 --> 01:33:04.070
(mumbles)

01:33:04.070 --> 01:33:06.272
- How did you arrive at that?

01:33:06.272 --> 01:33:08.240
- Hang on one second.

01:33:08.240 --> 01:33:09.970
Let's get Dale.

01:33:09.970 --> 01:33:11.220
Dale we're going to get you.

01:33:13.653 --> 01:33:17.510
- [Dale] There are however
in its public circumstances

01:33:19.891 --> 01:33:21.790
of so dreadful terrific in nature

01:33:21.790 --> 01:33:26.790
that most material alteration
in its a construction

01:33:28.840 --> 01:33:32.610
has been thought necessary which is

01:33:36.324 --> 01:33:40.230
hope has been affected without
injury either to the poetry

01:33:41.210 --> 01:33:43.890
or the intense and powerful interest

01:33:43.890 --> 01:33:46.233
which pervades the romantic. What I sense

01:33:50.760 --> 01:33:51.623
- Carry on.

01:33:53.070 --> 01:33:55.068
You can walk into the trap.

01:33:55.068 --> 01:33:57.151
(laughs)

01:33:58.123 --> 01:34:00.290
(mumbles)

01:34:06.250 --> 01:34:08.710
What would be the point in saying

01:34:08.710 --> 01:34:12.390
this is "The Mysterious Mother" by Walpole

01:34:12.390 --> 01:34:14.930
if it's circulating in the print culture

01:34:14.930 --> 01:34:17.020
which is what everybody's
been talking about

01:34:17.020 --> 01:34:20.760
so that everybody knows it's
circulated in the print culture

01:34:20.760 --> 01:34:24.800
so it is a book everybody's
been saying which is known

01:34:24.800 --> 01:34:29.800
and then you don't do it the
way that people have read it

01:34:30.680 --> 01:34:33.770
and so what you've got
really because the playbill

01:34:33.770 --> 01:34:38.770
is a mediation between the
performance and the audience.

01:34:40.690 --> 01:34:42.550
What you've run into the trap is,

01:34:42.550 --> 01:34:45.963
this is viewer caution advised.

01:34:46.904 --> 01:34:48.600
So that's the come on,

01:34:48.600 --> 01:34:50.830
oh you want to go and see that play

01:34:50.830 --> 01:34:53.800
because there's something going on in it.

01:34:53.800 --> 01:34:57.010
So would you have taken out the incest?

01:34:57.010 --> 01:34:58.870
Why produce that play?

01:34:58.870 --> 01:35:00.603
- [Male] I didn't mean
they're being taken out

01:35:00.603 --> 01:35:02.900
but I think it would have
been a version of the play

01:35:02.900 --> 01:35:05.286
closer to Mason's--

01:35:05.286 --> 01:35:08.800
- It's referring to
some sort of alteration

01:35:08.800 --> 01:35:10.130
and materially around the incest.

01:35:10.130 --> 01:35:13.050
- Which is the music, right?

01:35:13.050 --> 01:35:15.140
That's a material alteration.

01:35:15.140 --> 01:35:19.483
- I don't see any evidence
of that whatsover.

01:35:19.483 --> 01:35:21.330
It's been tidied up.

01:35:21.330 --> 01:35:25.150
Exactly and I agree entirely
that it's drawing attention

01:35:25.150 --> 01:35:27.860
to the scandalous nature of the play

01:35:27.860 --> 01:35:32.570
while perhaps avoiding
obscenity but it seems like

01:35:32.570 --> 01:35:33.460
that's the draw.

01:35:33.460 --> 01:35:36.780
Like you know there's
something like intensely dark

01:35:36.780 --> 01:35:41.528
and frightening about the incest plot

01:35:41.528 --> 01:35:45.780
we're going to stage this in
such a way that's legal

01:35:45.780 --> 01:35:47.500
and at the same time
we're going to remind you

01:35:47.500 --> 01:35:51.460
about everything that's so
treacherous about this play.

01:35:51.460 --> 01:35:53.150
- Kind of alcohol free wine.

01:35:54.370 --> 01:35:55.396
It's what you're saying.

01:35:55.396 --> 01:35:58.113
- The follow up question
in but then also a comment.

01:36:00.160 --> 01:36:02.290
In the reviews that you say you've seen,

01:36:02.290 --> 01:36:04.550
does anybody mentioned that
the incest is still there?

01:36:04.550 --> 01:36:05.935
- No.

01:36:05.935 --> 01:36:08.200
- They don't indicate either way?

01:36:08.200 --> 01:36:09.450
- They're having a good time

01:36:09.450 --> 01:36:11.170
just as the school boys were.

01:36:12.009 --> 01:36:14.270
That's my point is that and
they don't say anything.

01:36:14.270 --> 01:36:16.740
- Then the other question is precisely

01:36:16.740 --> 01:36:19.040
what is the nature of the
scandal? Because we've heard

01:36:19.040 --> 01:36:20.660
that there are all these incest things

01:36:20.660 --> 01:36:21.740
and I think it's it Emma Clery.

01:36:21.740 --> 01:36:23.860
I think Marcie talks about
this in one of her articles

01:36:23.860 --> 01:36:28.540
that one of the most scandalous
things about the play

01:36:28.540 --> 01:36:32.180
is the Mysterious Mother's obvious lust.

01:36:32.180 --> 01:36:34.610
That's more scandalous
than incest in itself.

01:36:34.610 --> 01:36:37.870
It's the desiring mother,
the actively desiring mother

01:36:37.870 --> 01:36:42.090
who kind of rapes her son
and that's the real scandal.

01:36:42.090 --> 01:36:44.826
Well that's more of a scandal than incest.

01:36:44.826 --> 01:36:45.709
- [Male] It's part of it.

01:36:45.709 --> 01:36:47.876
(mumbles)

01:36:49.810 --> 01:36:52.030
It's sexuality in one way or another.

01:36:52.030 --> 01:36:55.083
- And representing it is very
different from reading it.

01:36:56.544 --> 01:37:01.544
It's more powerful and so
reading it is a little safer.

01:37:01.750 --> 01:37:03.840
- I'm gonna take moderator
and director's privilege here

01:37:03.840 --> 01:37:06.870
and say that in this
work of representation

01:37:06.870 --> 01:37:08.780
we're back to some of the problems

01:37:08.780 --> 01:37:12.290
that Judith has set
out. And in the question

01:37:12.290 --> 01:37:15.889
that both Marcie and Jean
brought to the floor,

01:37:15.889 --> 01:37:19.790
did Walpole know this was unperformable

01:37:19.790 --> 01:37:22.680
and I would just take that question apart

01:37:22.680 --> 01:37:27.160
and make a distinction
between performable and public

01:37:27.160 --> 01:37:31.390
and I think that that's where
we can kind of find our way in

01:37:31.390 --> 01:37:36.293
and think about his imagined,
amateur and private audiences

01:37:37.270 --> 01:37:41.139
as a place where it is
indeed quite performable

01:37:41.139 --> 01:37:43.140
and the intimacy of those gatherings

01:37:43.140 --> 01:37:47.800
as we discovered in rehearsal
with a very small group

01:37:47.800 --> 01:37:50.460
elicits a lot of reaction.

01:37:50.460 --> 01:37:52.670
The ick and the gag reflexes.

01:37:52.670 --> 01:37:56.590
The sort of engagement
with production itself

01:37:56.590 --> 01:37:58.630
that's possible, that has something to do

01:37:58.630 --> 01:38:02.410
with scale of audience and
the intimacy of the gathering.

01:38:02.410 --> 01:38:05.430
So I think that's where we
can kind of take back apart

01:38:05.430 --> 01:38:08.070
the question about the
performability of it

01:38:08.070 --> 01:38:12.263
into the possibility of private
and amateur performance.

01:38:14.611 --> 01:38:16.400
- [Georgina] I will just add something,

01:38:16.400 --> 01:38:19.770
it's not that it's retrospective.

01:38:19.770 --> 01:38:23.820
If you don't see anything going
on between those two people,

01:38:23.820 --> 01:38:27.950
they report it so it's not
as offensive as actually

01:38:27.950 --> 01:38:30.690
I mean it's kind of French
in a way or a Greek even

01:38:30.690 --> 01:38:35.690
so you only see her
confessing to what she's done.

01:38:35.690 --> 01:38:39.620
And that there is physical
contact went in our production

01:38:39.620 --> 01:38:43.640
he caught her and you could say that

01:38:43.640 --> 01:38:47.790
he helped her stab
herself which is phallic

01:38:47.790 --> 01:38:50.324
but you wouldn't need to do that

01:38:50.324 --> 01:38:54.360
in a physical representation.

01:38:54.360 --> 01:38:57.240
You could do an awful lot with music

01:38:57.240 --> 01:39:01.620
which might help make
it a lot shorter as well

01:39:01.620 --> 01:39:05.380
and perhaps maybe a little
bit more entertaining

01:39:05.380 --> 01:39:06.910
because we did discover that some of it

01:39:06.910 --> 01:39:08.969
was a little bit funny.

01:39:08.969 --> 01:39:11.530
So there are two things
going on there as well

01:39:11.530 --> 01:39:15.410
and I think if you cut the incest out

01:39:15.410 --> 01:39:17.290
I mean you've got a very big problem

01:39:17.290 --> 01:39:18.540
with the whole play really.

01:39:18.540 --> 01:39:20.833
I mean that's the whole point.

01:39:22.667 --> 01:39:25.546
- [Male] But look at the title.

01:39:25.546 --> 01:39:26.574
(laughs)

01:39:26.574 --> 01:39:30.060
- The title alludes to
Jephson's "The Castle of Narbonne"

01:39:30.060 --> 01:39:32.180
which is already a sanitized version

01:39:32.180 --> 01:39:35.200
that only has name from...

01:39:36.160 --> 01:39:38.640
So it alludes to that with a bit sort

01:39:38.640 --> 01:39:42.383
couple of spoonfuls more of
Mysterious Mother in that.

01:39:43.320 --> 01:39:48.320
- One of the reviewers
refers to it mistitled

01:39:48.367 --> 01:39:50.688
it as "The Countess of Narbonne"

01:39:50.688 --> 01:39:52.593
which is really quite interesting.

01:39:53.610 --> 01:39:56.220
- Well and as we discovered too in a cut,

01:39:56.220 --> 01:39:59.003
music can do a great deal of work for you.

01:40:03.280 --> 01:40:06.323
- [Male] Could I ask this
is for Judith really.

01:40:08.440 --> 01:40:10.858
I was interested in I
really enjoyed your paper,

01:40:10.858 --> 01:40:15.210
the ties between the
way the Walpole controls

01:40:15.210 --> 01:40:18.440
the circulation of text
using the story on press

01:40:18.440 --> 01:40:20.630
and describe and decides very carefully

01:40:20.630 --> 01:40:22.480
whether it's published under his own name

01:40:23.327 --> 01:40:24.720
or published separately
but nothing resembling

01:40:24.720 --> 01:40:25.800
or not published at

01:40:28.010 --> 01:40:31.653
and how some things are printed
but not so fitted at all

01:40:32.655 --> 01:40:35.623
and same way with the
your ideas with the play

01:40:35.623 --> 01:40:39.040
is just how that's contained
to an amateur private group

01:40:39.040 --> 01:40:39.873
as we've said.

01:40:40.750 --> 01:40:41.910
It would have helped the argument

01:40:41.910 --> 01:40:44.422
if he would have been involved
in any sort of production

01:40:44.422 --> 01:40:46.450
but he actually isn't but it
does time to the other point

01:40:48.360 --> 01:40:50.633
which is the whole gentility business.

01:40:51.510 --> 01:40:55.180
If you were doing an amateur
theatrical by gentlemen

01:40:55.180 --> 01:40:58.590
there's that great line about how gentleman

01:40:58.590 --> 01:41:03.030
will react better or something like that.

01:41:03.030 --> 01:41:05.263
So there's all that class thing.

01:41:08.633 --> 01:41:09.650
- It's in my text,

01:41:09.650 --> 01:41:10.870
but I might not have read it out.

01:41:10.870 --> 01:41:14.040
So it makes it quite clear
that that there is no record

01:41:14.040 --> 01:41:16.667
of a private theatrical performance of it.

01:41:16.667 --> 01:41:21.667
And I don't have any record
of Walpole himself on stage

01:41:22.330 --> 01:41:25.970
but he certainly attends
private theatricals quite a lot

01:41:25.970 --> 01:41:30.970
and the way in which the
audience and the actors

01:41:31.070 --> 01:41:31.980
are drawn from the same class

01:41:31.980 --> 01:41:34.210
in a way that the
performance we saw yesterday.

01:41:34.210 --> 01:41:36.528
What we saw yesterday
was kind of an elite,

01:41:36.528 --> 01:41:39.570
amateur dramatic performance, wasn't it?

01:41:39.570 --> 01:41:43.110
Where you're the intellectual
aristocracy out there

01:41:43.110 --> 01:41:44.527
and you're the same kind of people

01:41:44.527 --> 01:41:47.820
from roughly the same group, aren't we?

01:41:47.820 --> 01:41:51.290
So that sort of circularity,
the mirror world

01:41:51.290 --> 01:41:52.460
of the audience [indistinct].

01:41:52.460 --> 01:41:55.210
So he participates in private
theatricals in that way

01:41:55.210 --> 01:41:57.093
but it wasn't performed.

01:41:58.059 --> 01:42:02.580
I think he probably did
feel it never anyway

01:42:02.580 --> 01:42:06.183
so there's that. On the printing of texts,

01:42:07.450 --> 01:42:10.150
you know much more about how
the printing press worked

01:42:10.150 --> 01:42:11.790
and the audience worked but I do think

01:42:11.790 --> 01:42:15.710
there's a way in which as you say

01:42:15.710 --> 01:42:16.880
sort of answered your own question,

01:42:16.880 --> 01:42:20.250
the choice of who he circulates things to,

01:42:20.250 --> 01:42:25.250
the amount of attention he
gives to the authorship.

01:42:25.290 --> 01:42:30.270
Some works are the author
is puffed up enormously

01:42:30.270 --> 01:42:32.340
or the press has its own agency,

01:42:32.340 --> 01:42:35.660
the press speaks, the press
takes on a life of its own

01:42:35.660 --> 01:42:38.100
so there's something much more personal

01:42:38.100 --> 01:42:42.710
non-commercial I think is
part of the point there.

01:42:42.710 --> 01:42:44.640
These are things given as gifts,

01:42:44.640 --> 01:42:49.640
as part of a culture of exchange
not a marketplace culture.

01:42:50.090 --> 01:42:53.910
And definitely I think the
point about the actors,

01:42:53.910 --> 01:42:57.380
I forgot to read a passage
just very very briefly.

01:42:57.380 --> 01:43:00.330
A short quotation from his description

01:43:00.330 --> 01:43:04.240
of a performance with the Hobart's in it

01:43:04.240 --> 01:43:06.240
and I was quite surprised that he has

01:43:06.240 --> 01:43:08.100
this very favorable
account of Mrs. Hobart.

01:43:08.100 --> 01:43:13.100
You might know Albania Hobart
from Gilray's satirical prints

01:43:13.550 --> 01:43:16.264
where he basically he
draws a complete circle

01:43:16.264 --> 01:43:18.570
and puts a tiny little circle
on top, that's her head.

01:43:18.570 --> 01:43:23.354
She was a woman of substantial means.

01:43:23.354 --> 01:43:26.790
And he describes a 1782 production

01:43:26.790 --> 01:43:30.250
of "Mirth is all in the
wrong" at Ham Common:

01:43:30.250 --> 01:43:33.560
"Mrs. Hobart played
admirably and most genteely

01:43:33.560 --> 01:43:36.090
which was very refreshing
as one never sees anything

01:43:36.090 --> 01:43:38.510
like a woman of fashion on the stage.

01:43:38.510 --> 01:43:41.160
The eldest Miss Hobart
so lovely and so modest

01:43:41.160 --> 01:43:44.450
was not acting, she was the thing itself."

01:43:44.450 --> 01:43:46.390
So there's a sense that
there's authenticity of it

01:43:46.390 --> 01:43:51.390
and you get this also and
Lady Craven's plays that

01:43:51.448 --> 01:43:55.810
if you're acting a tragedy
or you're acting a drama

01:43:55.810 --> 01:43:59.660
of public importance you have those people

01:43:59.660 --> 01:44:01.660
who play those roles in real life,

01:44:01.660 --> 01:44:05.981
playing the role of King
the Earl of so and so

01:44:05.981 --> 01:44:06.814
the Duke of so and so.

01:44:06.814 --> 01:44:08.560
So this again is that less

01:44:08.560 --> 01:44:11.480
there is a certain kind of authenticity

01:44:11.480 --> 01:44:16.208
and a certain kind of
campness combined in this

01:44:16.208 --> 01:44:18.350
to bring in sort of Marcie's point about

01:44:18.350 --> 01:44:20.810
this of the complexity the double nature

01:44:20.810 --> 01:44:24.870
of Walpole's attitude to most things.

01:44:24.870 --> 01:44:28.223
I hope that encapsulates that a bit.

01:44:33.560 --> 01:44:36.520
There were a number of complaints about

01:44:39.030 --> 01:44:40.310
what the private theatricals

01:44:40.310 --> 01:44:43.373
sort of take another slightly different view of it.

01:44:44.626 --> 01:44:47.100
There's a number of examples

01:44:47.100 --> 01:44:49.780
of people praising they very fine

01:44:49.780 --> 01:44:52.950
and authentic performance.

01:44:52.950 --> 01:44:55.280
There are also complaints
that the aristocrats

01:44:55.280 --> 01:44:58.910
were taking bread from their
professional actors mouths.

01:44:58.910 --> 01:45:01.670
That they're taking their jobs

01:45:01.670 --> 01:45:04.290
and also stealing their audiences.

01:45:04.290 --> 01:45:06.070
So when productions were done in London

01:45:06.070 --> 01:45:10.840
during the theatrical season,
Sheridan basically closed down

01:45:10.840 --> 01:45:13.130
one of the privates
theaters, The Picnic Society

01:45:13.130 --> 01:45:15.083
because they were taking his audience.

01:45:16.060 --> 01:45:17.605
- Jill.

01:45:17.605 --> 01:45:19.800
- [Jill] Thank you so
much for these papers.

01:45:19.800 --> 01:45:22.770
They're just so informative
and thought-provoking

01:45:22.770 --> 01:45:25.131
and I have two kind of
two unrelated things

01:45:25.131 --> 01:45:27.672
that I wanted to say or ask about.

01:45:27.672 --> 01:45:32.672
Such a revelatory basic
point about C"astle of Otranto"

01:45:32.690 --> 01:45:36.300
versus the world of "The Mysterious Mother"

01:45:36.300 --> 01:45:39.620
that there aren't
supernatural forces there.

01:45:39.620 --> 01:45:44.070
And I think about the
the Radcliff monk, Lewis

01:45:45.000 --> 01:45:48.990
kind of struggle and so on
the rationalized supernatural.

01:45:48.990 --> 01:45:51.070
But I was just thinking
looking at it through

01:45:51.070 --> 01:45:52.890
the light that you shed on it

01:45:54.980 --> 01:45:58.030
and also thinking about Nicole
Garret's paper this morning

01:45:58.960 --> 01:46:03.960
that the countess is read
as suspect and transgressive

01:46:05.330 --> 01:46:08.173
because she doesn't believe
in the supernatural.

01:46:09.040 --> 01:46:12.490
She's accused of that and then
she knows and we eventually

01:46:12.490 --> 01:46:14.870
know that no that's not the
nature of her crime at all.

01:46:14.870 --> 01:46:19.770
She has this this terrible past crime sin

01:46:19.770 --> 01:46:23.520
that she has committed and I
was just playing with the sense

01:46:23.520 --> 01:46:27.700
that Walpole was doing
something transgressive perhaps

01:46:27.700 --> 01:46:29.700
in the terms of the play

01:46:29.700 --> 01:46:33.260
in not extending faith
to the supernatural.

01:46:33.260 --> 01:46:37.620
I don't know how the
sexual crime and belief

01:46:37.620 --> 01:46:39.260
or disbelief in the supernatural

01:46:39.260 --> 01:46:41.130
and I'll just throw out
while I have the mic

01:46:41.130 --> 01:46:44.796
at my unrelated so just
to David and maybe others,

01:46:44.796 --> 01:46:47.760
Marcie or Julia could comment.

01:46:47.760 --> 01:46:49.700
I was just really struck with that letter

01:46:49.700 --> 01:46:53.310
from George Montagu which
keeps talking about the boys.

01:46:53.310 --> 01:46:54.980
You gave the birth dates.

01:46:54.980 --> 01:46:56.363
They're not boys.

01:46:58.154 --> 01:47:00.970
- It's a kind of homo
social group, isn't it

01:47:00.970 --> 01:47:03.770
- [Jill] In terms of
this a different aspect

01:47:03.770 --> 01:47:08.770
of the spontaneous reach
staged reading there.

01:47:11.349 --> 01:47:14.943
The kind of fantasy on
juvenileness of the group.

01:47:16.390 --> 01:47:18.140
So just throw those out for anyone.

01:47:19.480 --> 01:47:21.590
- To respond to your first point,

01:47:21.590 --> 01:47:25.170
it's kind of remarkable that
in these two texts by Walpole

01:47:25.170 --> 01:47:28.327
you see this sort of fundamental
tension in the Gothic.

01:47:28.327 --> 01:47:31.533
We want to sort of explore this.

01:47:33.330 --> 01:47:35.150
There's a fun little disagreement about

01:47:35.150 --> 01:47:36.650
to what extent should we assume

01:47:36.650 --> 01:47:38.150
that there's supernatural force.

01:47:38.150 --> 01:47:41.241
Do we problematize that
or do we play with it?

01:47:41.241 --> 01:47:43.913
And I don't know I mean would have read

01:47:43.913 --> 01:47:47.710
the countess's problem is
not that she is a heretic

01:47:47.710 --> 01:47:49.510
by refusing to believe
in the supernatural,

01:47:49.510 --> 01:47:54.420
or refusing to believe in the
magical power of confession

01:47:54.420 --> 01:47:57.665
to enact a miracle to
wipe your soul clean.

01:47:57.665 --> 01:47:59.390
I guess I didn't think of it that way.

01:47:59.390 --> 01:48:02.727
I thought it was just a
matter of her just resisting

01:48:02.727 --> 01:48:05.390
the authority that
she'd have to give over.

01:48:05.390 --> 01:48:08.360
So she knows as soon as
she speaks her truth,

01:48:08.360 --> 01:48:12.190
she's basically going to be manipulated,

01:48:12.190 --> 01:48:13.970
subject to blackmail but I think

01:48:13.970 --> 01:48:16.050
that is an interesting point

01:48:16.050 --> 01:48:18.350
and they certainly accuse her

01:48:18.350 --> 01:48:20.523
of not being faithful in that way.

01:48:21.360 --> 01:48:24.990
It's interesting that that position

01:48:24.990 --> 01:48:28.130
is put in the mouths of fools and children.

01:48:28.130 --> 01:48:30.320
And it's put in the mouths
of you know explicitly

01:48:30.320 --> 01:48:32.453
cynical manipulative priests.

01:48:34.818 --> 01:48:36.970
You read the play and you're
kind of supposed to take

01:48:36.970 --> 01:48:39.900
this sort of you know
demystified perspective on this.

01:48:39.900 --> 01:48:43.760
Of course it's not really
you know a supernatural event

01:48:43.760 --> 01:48:46.520
and we ought to...

01:48:46.520 --> 01:48:48.410
There are way more interesting

01:48:48.410 --> 01:48:50.130
sort of gothic themes to explore

01:48:50.130 --> 01:48:53.320
rather than is this world full of ghosts

01:48:53.320 --> 01:48:56.653
and vengeful gods and things like that.

01:48:58.588 --> 01:49:02.280
I guess in my thinking about
it this sort of engaged

01:49:02.280 --> 01:49:03.860
in this sort of lightning controversy,

01:49:03.860 --> 01:49:07.640
and kind of a larger sort of
enthusiasm but also suspicion

01:49:07.640 --> 01:49:10.280
of this enlightenment science project

01:49:10.280 --> 01:49:13.170
and in particular the way
in which it comes home,

01:49:13.170 --> 01:49:16.050
there's so much of interest
in doing one's own experiments.

01:49:16.050 --> 01:49:19.817
It becomes itself a
kind of private theater

01:49:19.817 --> 01:49:22.650
sort of performance discourse that folks

01:49:22.650 --> 01:49:24.420
sort of participate in.

01:49:24.420 --> 01:49:26.910
I guess I was thinking that Walpole

01:49:26.910 --> 01:49:30.260
is sort of picking up a similar ambiguity.

01:49:30.260 --> 01:49:33.120
Why are folks doing this?

01:49:33.120 --> 01:49:36.860
Are they really learning
some substantial truths

01:49:36.860 --> 01:49:37.873
about the universe?

01:49:39.105 --> 01:49:40.800
Are we really sort of engaged
in the project enlightenment,

01:49:40.800 --> 01:49:42.840
are we just thrilling to this stuff

01:49:42.840 --> 01:49:44.550
and kind of enjoying the sort of

01:49:44.550 --> 01:49:46.770
confusion delight and surprise.

01:49:46.770 --> 01:49:48.920
I feel like it's kind of a similar menu

01:49:48.920 --> 01:49:51.640
of ambiguous pleasures that you partake

01:49:51.640 --> 01:49:54.580
of those science performances as you would

01:49:54.580 --> 01:49:56.563
from this gothic performance.

01:49:57.930 --> 01:50:01.070
- So just to what you
were saying Jill before,

01:50:01.070 --> 01:50:03.140
I think what's really interesting is that

01:50:03.140 --> 01:50:04.610
what we see in the reception of the play

01:50:04.610 --> 01:50:06.610
where it's non reception or whatever

01:50:06.610 --> 01:50:09.710
is a range of sociable theaters.

01:50:09.710 --> 01:50:12.580
Some are more professional,
some are more private

01:50:12.580 --> 01:50:15.210
and some can be more spontaneous or not

01:50:15.210 --> 01:50:19.981
and they are all it's
quite an interesting range

01:50:19.981 --> 01:50:24.360
and I'm thinking myself of
Abigail Williams' recent book

01:50:24.360 --> 01:50:26.800
about sociable reading
where she talks about

01:50:26.800 --> 01:50:28.612
reading drama as well.

01:50:28.612 --> 01:50:32.480
I think that that's an
element of literary reception

01:50:32.480 --> 01:50:37.480
that we have a lot more work
to do on capturing the range.

01:50:39.110 --> 01:50:40.823
So that's that.

01:50:43.160 --> 01:50:45.280
- Nicole, I think we have time.

01:50:45.280 --> 01:50:48.320
- [Nicole] Thank you for
these really rich papers.

01:50:48.320 --> 01:50:51.460
My question is for Jeanne
and I was wondering,

01:50:51.460 --> 01:50:55.630
I remember reading Clery's
article on "The Mysterious Mother"

01:50:55.630 --> 01:51:00.040
or her contribution to the edited volume

01:51:00.040 --> 01:51:04.420
and she says or she claims
that audiences at the time

01:51:04.420 --> 01:51:07.060
would have noticed these resonances

01:51:07.060 --> 01:51:08.680
with earlier incest plays

01:51:08.680 --> 01:51:10.340
and wouldn't have been shocked by it

01:51:10.340 --> 01:51:13.020
and she particularly
calls attention to plays

01:51:13.020 --> 01:51:18.020
such as Dryden's collaboration
from Nathaniel Lee the 1679,

01:51:18.340 --> 01:51:21.390
Adaptation of Oedipus but she doesn't give

01:51:21.390 --> 01:51:26.390
any kind of textural sense of
actual evidence of reception

01:51:26.870 --> 01:51:28.400
which would indicate that readers

01:51:28.400 --> 01:51:31.280
were reading those plays together.

01:51:31.280 --> 01:51:35.233
So I was wondering if you come
across any evidence of that.

01:51:36.620 --> 01:51:39.603
- I don't know about
reading the plays together.

01:51:40.760 --> 01:51:45.760
Certainly just the responses for example

01:51:46.180 --> 01:51:48.080
to "Phaedra and Hippolytus"

01:51:49.560 --> 01:51:54.560
when would see more
complaints about Phaedra

01:51:55.120 --> 01:52:00.120
and her desires in contrast to say

01:52:00.650 --> 01:52:05.650
what they saw in Aureng-zebe
or in earlier plays

01:52:07.550 --> 01:52:12.550
in Greek versions of say the
Phaedra story or Oedipus even.

01:52:15.840 --> 01:52:20.200
So that they were aware of other plays

01:52:21.550 --> 01:52:24.300
and I'm not sure in some
cases from having read them

01:52:24.300 --> 01:52:25.970
in other cases from having seen them

01:52:25.970 --> 01:52:29.960
because the response to
"Phaedra and Hippolyta"

01:52:29.960 --> 01:52:34.370
is a really very much response
to seeing something staged

01:52:34.370 --> 01:52:35.783
and spoken about.

01:52:36.860 --> 01:52:39.197
Walpole actually when
he's talking about Phaedra

01:52:39.197 --> 01:52:42.630
and I think he's talking about
Phaedra talking to the chorus

01:52:42.630 --> 01:52:46.850
in the Greek play is talking about

01:52:46.850 --> 01:52:48.870
how illogical it is.

01:52:48.870 --> 01:52:53.870
The responses that say
Gildon had to Smith's play

01:52:54.430 --> 01:52:59.430
are about staging it and
hearing Phaedra vocalize

01:53:00.780 --> 01:53:05.410
the fact that she desires
her son and that's...

01:53:05.410 --> 01:53:10.410
So I think that there is 
an awareness of this body.

01:53:11.520 --> 01:53:15.250
I mean certainly nobody could
avoid knowing "The Orphan"

01:53:15.250 --> 01:53:17.670
was one of the hugely popular plays

01:53:17.670 --> 01:53:19.920
when you get to "The Old Manor House,"

01:53:19.920 --> 01:53:24.540
of course the character
of the orphan is Monimia

01:53:24.540 --> 01:53:29.540
so I think that in general that
these were the popular ones

01:53:31.250 --> 01:53:33.913
were just lurking in people's minds.

01:53:34.960 --> 01:53:38.500
I wouldn't give that
claim say to "Don Sebastian"

01:53:38.500 --> 01:53:43.500
or necessarily as you
got further along to say

01:53:43.745 --> 01:53:46.080
"Phaedra and Hippolytus"
although it was staged

01:53:46.080 --> 01:53:48.463
multiple times in the 1750s.

01:53:49.530 --> 01:53:53.160
So it's really hard
unless you can trace down

01:53:54.130 --> 01:53:56.723
going through a journal
or finding letters,

01:53:56.723 --> 01:53:58.700
just have somebody as well I saw this

01:53:58.700 --> 01:54:01.060
and it made me think of

01:54:03.370 --> 01:54:05.223
seeing such and such a play.

01:54:06.660 --> 01:54:08.163
So I don't know if that helps.

01:54:09.820 --> 01:54:11.210
- I'm afraid we're out of time

01:54:11.210 --> 01:54:14.940
but I want to thank everyone
from the Lewis Walpole library

01:54:14.940 --> 01:54:17.020
who has made this possible,

01:54:17.020 --> 01:54:20.390
everyone from the Yale
Center for British Art

01:54:20.390 --> 01:54:22.110
especially Dean up in the back

01:54:22.110 --> 01:54:25.660
and thank you all for taking
a chance on a crazy experiment

01:54:25.660 --> 01:54:29.370
to stage this play which I
think we've learned a great deal

01:54:29.370 --> 01:54:31.690
and thanks to our panelists and everyone

01:54:31.690 --> 01:54:33.360
for their participation.

01:54:33.360 --> 01:54:35.007
Have a great afternoon.

01:54:35.007 --> 01:54:37.257
(applause)

01:54:40.028 --> 01:54:42.528
(light music)

